			<article id="art3" class="article-card">
				<h1 class="articleNumber"><b>ARTICLE 3</b></h1>
				<h1 class="articleTitle">RIGHTS AND RESPONSIBILITIES OF A CHILD</h1>

				<section id="art3-sec10" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 10. Rights of the Child. — <span class="section-top">Every child is endowed with inherent rights that the Municipal Government of Libacao is sworn to protect and uphold. These rights shall be enjoyed by every child without distinction as to legitimacy or illegitimacy, sex, social status, religion, political antecedents, or any other factor. These rights are classified into the following four pillars:</span></h2>
					
					<p id="art3-sec10.1" style="font-weight:bolder;">10.1. Survival Rights</p>
					<p><b>Survival Rights</b> ensure the child’s inherent right to life and to the needs that are most basic to existence, including the right to a name and nationality, the right to identity, and those dealing with parental and governmental duties and obligations, such as an adequate and decent standard of living, access to basic healthcare and medical services, social security, and rehabilitation. The following are the survival rights:</p>
					<div style="margin-left: 2rem">
						<p><b>a) Right to be Born Well.</b> Every child has the right to be born well, endowed with the dignity and worth of a human being from the moment of his/her conception;</p>
						<p><b>b) Right to Identity.</b> Every child has the right to a name, nationality, and filiation to establish his/her identity with the rest of the world;</p>
						<p><b>c) Right to Family Life.</b> Every child has the right to a wholesome family life that will provide him/her with love, care, understanding, guidance and counseling, and moral and material security;</p>
						<p><b>d) Right to Basic Needs.</b> Every child has the right to a balanced diet, adequate clothing, sufficient shelter, proper medical attention, and all the basic physical requirements of a healthy and vigorous life.</p>
					</div>

					<p id="art3-sec10.2" style="font-weight:bolder;">10.2. Development Rights</p>
					<p><b>Development Rights</b> refer to the rights of a child to education and to develop his/her personality, talents, and mental and physical abilities to the fullest extent. These also include participation in cultural activities, access to appropriate and relevant information, and opportunities for rest, play, and leisure. The following are the development rights:</p>
					<div style="margin-left: 2rem">
						<p><b>a) Right to Full Development.</b> Every child has the right to a well-rounded development of his/her personality to the end that he/she may become a happy, useful, and active member of society. Specifically:</p>
							<div style="margin-left: 2rem">
								<p>1)	The gifted child shall be given the opportunity and encouragement to develop his/her special talents;</p>
								<p>2)	The emotionally disturbed or socially maladjusted child shall be treated with sympathy and understanding, and shall be entitled to treatment and competent care;</p>
								<p>3)	The physically or mentally disabled child shall be given treatment and care required by his/her particular condition;</p>
							</div>
						<p><b>b) Right to Moral Atmosphere.</b> Every child has the right to be brought up in an atmosphere of morality and rectitude for the enrichment and the strengthening of his/her character;</p>
						<p><b>c) Right to a Peaceful Environment.</b> Every child has the right to grow up as a free individual in an atmosphere of peace, understanding, tolerance, and universal sisterhood/brotherhood, and with the determination to contribute his/her share in the building of a better world;</p>
						<p><b>d) Right to Education.</b> Every child has the right to education. Primary education must be compulsory and higher education must be available and accessible to all on the basis of capacity by every appropriate means. The education of the child must be directed to:</p>
							<div style="margin-left: 2rem">
								<p>1)	The development of the child’s personality, moral values, talents, and mental and physical abilities to the fullest extent;</p>
								<p>2)	The preparation of the child for responsible adult life in a free society;</p>
								<p>3)	The development of respect for parents, his/her cultural identity, language, and values, and the cultural background and culture of others;</p>
								<p>4)	The development of respect for the natural environment;</p>
							</div>
						<p><b>e) Right to Recreation.</b> Every child has the right to full opportunities for safe and wholesome recreation and activities, individual as well as social, for the wholesome use of his/her leisure hours;</p>
						<p><b>f) Right to a Conducive Community.</b> Every child has the right to live in a community and a society that can offer him/her an environment free from pernicious influences and conducive to the promotion of his/her health and the cultivation of his/her desirable traits and attributes.</p>
					</div>

					<p id="art3-sec10.3" style="font-weight:bolder;">10.3. Protection Rights</p>
					<p><b>Protection Rights</b> cover those rights protecting the child from all forms of abuses and discrimination such as protection from cruelty, torture, arbitrary separation from family, abuses in the justice and penal system, involvement in armed conflict, child labor, drug abuse, sexual abuse, and exploitation. The following are the protection rights:</p>
					<div style="margin-left: 2rem">
						<p><b>a) Right to Safety from Abuse.</b> Every child shall be protected from all forms of physical or mental violence, injury, cruelty, torture, neglect, or negligent treatment, including sexual abuse and exploitation;</p>
						<p><b>b) Protection from Economic Exploitation.</b> Every child shall be protected from child labor and from performing any work that is likely to be hazardous or to interfere with his/her education or development;</p>
						<p><b>c) Protection from Substance Abuse.</b> Every child shall be protected from the use of narcotic drugs and psychotropic substances, and from being used in the illicit production and trafficking of such substances;</p>
						<p><b>d) Protection in Situations of Armed Conflict.</b> Every child shall be protected from recruitment into armed groups and from the effects of hostilities. In times of armed conflict, children shall be considered "Zones of Peace" and shall be provided with special care and protection;</p>
						<p><b>e) Protection in the Justice System.</b> Every child shall be protected from abuses within the justice and penal system. No child shall be subjected to torture or other cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment or punishment. Any child in conflict with the law (CICL) shall be treated with humanity and respect for his/her inherent dignity;</p>
						<p><b>f) Right to Family Integrity.</b> Every child shall be protected against arbitrary or unlawful separation from his/her parents, except when such separation is necessary for the best interests of the child.</p>
					</div>

					<p id="art3-sec10.4" style="font-weight:bolder;">10.4. Participation Rights</p>
					<p><b>Participation Rights</b> refer to the child’s rights to participate in matters that affect him/her most by providing all appropriate venues where he/she can express his/her opinions freely and to have these opinions taken into account, involvement in decision-making and consultative process, freedom of association, and peaceful assembly. The following are the participation rights:</p>
					<div style="margin-left: 2rem">
						<p><b>a) Right to be Heard in Proceedings.</b> Every child has the right to be provided the opportunity to be heard in any judicial and administrative proceedings affecting him/her, either directly, or through a representative or an appropriate body, in a manner consistent with the procedural rules of national law;</p>
						<p><b>b) Freedom of Thought, Conscience, and Religion.</b> Every child has the right to freedom of thought, conscience, and religion, subject to appropriate parental guidance;</p>
						<p><b>c) Freedom of Statement.</b> Every child has the right to freedom of statement. This right shall include freedom to seek, receive, and impart information and ideas of all kinds;</p>
						<p><b>d) Right to Express Opinions.</b> Every child has the right to express his/her opinions freely and to have these opinions taken into account in any matter or procedure affecting him/her;</p>
						<p><b>e) Right to Privacy and Honor.</b> Every child has the right to privacy and shall be protected against unlawful interference with such privacy, family, home, or correspondence, and against unlawful attacks against his/her honor and reputation.</p>
					</div>
				</section>

				<section id="art3-sec11" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 11. Responsibilities of the Child – <span class="section-top">Every child, regardless of the circumstances of his/her birth, sex, religion, social status, political antecedents, and other factors, shall:</span></h2>
					<p><b></b></p>
						<div style="margin-left: 2rem">
							<p><b>a) Lead a Virtuous Life.</b> Strive to lead an upright and virtuous life in accordance with the tenets of his/her religion, the teachings of his/her elders and mentors, and the biddings of a clean conscience;</p>
							<p><b>b) Honor the Family.</b> Love, respect, and obey his/her parents, and cooperate with them in the strengthening of the family;</p>
							<p><b>c) Promote Family Harmony.</b> Extend to his/her brothers and sisters love, thoughtfulness, and helpfulness, and endeavor with them to keep the family harmonious and united;</p>
							<p><b>d) Respect Authority and Heritage.</b> Respect his/her elders and guardians, as well as the customs and traditions of the community, the memory of our national heroes, the duly constituted authorities, the laws of the country, and the principles and institutions of democracy;</p>
							<p><b>e) Pursue Personal Development.</b> Exert his/her utmost capacity to develop his/her potentialities for service, particularly by undergoing a formal education suited to his/her abilities, in order that he/she may become an asset to himself/herself and to society;</p>
							<p><b>f) Engage in Civic Affairs.</b> Actively participate in socio-civic affairs, the protection of the environment, and the promotion of the general welfare, recognizing that the youth will eventually be called upon to discharge the responsibility of leadership in shaping the nation’s future;</p>
							<p><b>g) Uphold Global Peace.</b> Help in the observance of individual human rights, the strengthening of freedom, the fostering of cooperation among nations in the pursuit of their common aspiration for unity and prosperity, and the furtherance of world peace; and</p>
							<p><b>h) Respect Indigenous Culture.</b> Fulfill his/her responsibilities toward the preservation and practice of indigenous culture and traditions, <span style="color: lightcoral;">**TODO TO BE coordinated with the MSWDO and the Indigenous Peoples Mandatory Representative (IPMR). /TODO**</span></p>
						</div>
				</section>
			</article>

			<!-- Article 4 Content -->
			<article id="art4" class="article-card">
				<h1 class="articleNumber"><b>ARTICLE 4</b></h1>
				<h1 class="articleTitle">ROLES AND FUNCTION OF VARIOUS SECTORS</h1>
				<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>

				<section id="art4-sec12" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 12. The Family. —<span class="section-top"> The family is the basic and central unit of society responsible for the primary socialization of children, which is essential to the prevention of children’s rights violations. The Municipal Government shall pursue all efforts to preserve the integrity of the family, including the extended family.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
				</section>

				<section id="art4-sec13" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 13. Primary Duties and Rights of Parents. —<span class="section-top"> Parents have the primary duty of nurturing and protecting children from infancy to adolescence. The introduction of children to the culture, values, and norms of society shall begin in the family. Parents shall have the right to the company of his/her children and, in relation to all persons or institutions dealing with the child’s development, the primary right and obligation to provide for his/her upbringing.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art4-sec14" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 14. Rights Under the Family Code. —<span class="section-top"> Parents shall continue to exercise the rights mentioned in Articles 209 to 237 of the Family Code of the Philippines over the person and property of their children.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art4-sec15" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 15. Positive Discipline and Formation of Character. —<span class="section-top"> Parents shall have the right and duty to discipline his/her children as may be necessary for the formation of his/her good character. In exercising this right, parents shall utilize Positive and Non-Violent Discipline, focusing on respect, guidance, and the child's developmental stage. Parents may require from their children obedience to just and reasonable rules, suggestions, and admonitions; provided, that in no case shall corporal punishment or any act that is physically, mentally, or emotionally abusive be used as a form of discipline.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art4-sec16" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 16. General Duties of Parents. —<span class="section-top"> Parents shall have the following general duties toward their children:</span></h2>
					<div style="margin-left: 2rem">
						<p><b>a) Registration.</b> To provide the child a name and nationality by registering the newborn with the Office of the Local Civil Registrar where the birth occurred;</p>
						<p><b>b) Moral and Spiritual Guidance.</b> To provide moral and spiritual guidance, teaching the child to lead an upright life, to respect the rights of others, and to fulfill his/her duties to the community and the State;</p>
						<p><b>c) Basic Support.</b> To provide for basic needs, including but not limited to dwelling, clothing, medical attendance, and education, as mandated by Article 194 of the Family Code;</p>
						<p><b>d) Protection.</b> To provide protection against all forms of child labor, abuse, neglect, and other forms of violence;</p>
						<p><b>e) Maternal and Child Health.</b> To ensure at least four (4) pre-natal and two (2) post-natal medical check-ups for the mother;</p>
						<p><b>f) Safe Birth.</b> To encourage and prioritize facility-based delivery for the safety of both mother and child;</p>
						<p><b>g) Newborn Screening.</b> To submit the child for Newborn Screening within 24 to 48 hours of life for early detection of health conditions;</p>
						<p><b>h) Nutrition.</b> To initiate and sustain breastfeeding and provide appropriate complementary feeding until the child is at least two (2) years old;</p>
						<p><b>i) Immunization.</b> To submit their children for complete immunization (Fully Immunized Child/MRSH) as required by national health programs;</p>
						<p><b>j) Food Security.</b> To ensure nutritional health through proper counseling and the provision of diverse, age-appropriate food; and</p>
						<p><b>k) Regular Monitoring.</b> To ensure the child undergoes regular health check-ups and weight monitoring (Operation Timbang/OPT) and receives Integrated Management of Childhood Illness (IMCI) care when necessary.</p>
					</div>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art4-sec17" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 17. Role of Mass Media. —<span class="section-top"> The mass media shall be aware of their extensive social role and responsibility, as well as their influence, in communications relating to children. They shall use their power to protect the rights of children by relaying consistent messages through a balanced approach and provide protection to victims from undue publicity, ensuring the confidentiality of proceedings and the identity of the child in accordance with Section 22 of RA 7610 and RA 9344.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art4-sec18" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 18. Role of Judicial and Legal Institutions. —<span class="section-top"> In the administration of justice, the courts, prosecutors, the Public Attorney’s Office (PAO), the Integrated Bar of the Philippines (IBP), and other judicial or legal sectors shall ensure that the rights of children are protected and promoted at all times. They shall conduct programs and activities geared towards enhancing children’s rights and ensuring that the justice system remains child-sensitive and restorative.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art4-sec19" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 19. Role of the Local Government Unit. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipal Government of Libacao shall allocate and prioritize funds for the mandatory one percent (1%) LCPC budget, Gender and Development (GAD) budget, and other development funds for the immediate, effective, and sustainable implementation of children-related programs and projects.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art4-sec20" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 20. Role of Municipal Council for the Protection of Children (MCPC). —<span class="section-top"> The MCPC shall, in addition to its existing duties and functions, coordinate with and assist the LGU in formulating comprehensive programs for children. It shall serve as the primary body to oversee, monitor, and evaluate the implementation of such programs.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art4-sec21" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 21. Role of the Sangguniang Kabataan (SK). —<span class="section-top"> The Sangguniang Kabataan, as established under the Local Government Code and the SK Reform Act (RA 10742), shall actively involve itself in the formulation and implementation of prevention and protection programs in the community. It shall coordinate with the Local Councils for the Protection of Children for this purpose and is mandated to appropriate funds for programs, projects, and activities that benefit the youth and children in the municipality.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art4-sec22" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 22. Role of Civil Society Organizations (CSOs). —<span class="section-top"> Civil Society Organizations operating in the Municipality of Libacao are encouraged to re-focus or realign their programs and projects toward the protection of children in collaboration with the government and other child-focused institutions.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>
			</article>

			<!-- Article 5 Content -->
			<article id="art5" class="article-card">
				<h1 class="articleNumber"><b>ARTICLE 5</b></h1>
				<h1 class="articleTitle">PROGRAMS AND SERVICES FOR CHILDREN</h1>

				<p style="font-weight:bolder; font-size:larger;">A. Comprehensive Programs for Children</p>

				<section id="art5-sec23" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 23. Comprehensive Program for Children. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipality shall, within one (1) year from the effectivity of this Code and every three (3) years thereafter, formulate a comprehensive program for children covering at least a three-year period. Such program shall include prevention, protection, and rehabilitation programs, and shall emphasize the prevention of rights violations, including child abuse, drug addiction, child labor, teenage pregnancy, child marriage, and other issues affecting the youth.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec24" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 24. The Community Approach. —<span class="section-top"> The community approach shall be given special attention in the comprehensive program for children. This involves addressing community-wide issues and improving the overall environment to prevent violations of children’s rights through the mobilization of human resources within the community. Volunteerism among community members to assist in implementation shall be encouraged. Community-based services and programs that respond to the special needs, problems, interests and concerns of young persons and offer appropriate counseling to their family shall be developed or strengthened.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec25" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 25. Process in Formulating Comprehensive Children’s Programs. —<span class="section-top"> The process shall be participatory and consultative. The Municipality of Libacao, in coordination with the MCPC, shall involve all sectors concerned: child-focused institutions, CSOs, People’s Organizations, Children’s Councils, Educational Institutions, and Government Agencies involved with children’s concerns like the Department of Social Welfare and Development (DSWD), Department of Health (DOH), PNP-WCPD and the Department of Education (DepEd) to participate in the planning process.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top">The LGU shall ensure that children and youth themselves participate in the formulation, development, and implementation of these programs, most particularly in identifying their needs.</span></h2>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec26" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 26. Periodic Review and Assessment. —<span class="section-top"> The programs shall be reviewed and assessed yearly by the Municipality in coordination with the Local Councils for the Protection of Children regarding their effectiveness based on identified indicators. Programs may be adjusted if necessary.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec27" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">B. Programs to Prevent Children’s Rights Violation</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 27. Prevention Programs. —<span class="section-top"> Prevention programs shall be an important component of the Comprehensive Programs for Children.  These shall consist of three levels:</span></h2>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Primary Prevention:</b> General measures to promote social justice and equal opportunity tackling root causes of children’s rights violations like poverty and other forms of marginalization;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Secondary Prevention:</b> Measures to assist children identified as "more at risk", such as those whose parents are in special difficulty or are not caring appropriately for them.</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Tertiary Prevention:</b> Schemes to prevent children’s rights violations from recurring.</p>
						<p class="section-inner">In all levels of prevention, official intervention shall be guided by fairness and equity, always considering the best interest of the child.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec28" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">C. Other Proactive Programs</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top">1. Parenting Orientation Courses</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 28. Parenting Orientation Courses. —<span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a)</b> Courses may be integrated into the JHS/SHS curriculum through DepEd rules and regulations.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b)</b> Courses may be incorporated into the Parent-Teacher Association (PTA) Alternative Learning System.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c)</b> Marriage license applicants shall participate in <b>Parenting Orientation Course I (Gender and Child Sensitivity)</b> prior to the issuance of a license by the Local Civil Registrar. This course shall become an integral part of existing family planning seminars or reproductive health courses. The MCPC, MHO, and Civil Registrar shall update the design of family planning seminars to conform with this Ordinance, and the mechanics of its implementation shall be specifically formulated in its implementing Rules and Regulations.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d)</b> Parents/guardians shall participate in <b>Parenting Orientation Course II</b> during the school year a child is enrolled in a Day Care Center in Libacao.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e)</b> The <b>Parent Effectiveness Service (PES)</b> in all barangays shall be vigorously pursued to help in developing stronger family ties.</p>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b>The Municipal Council for the Protection of Children (MCPC) and the Office of the Municipal Social Welfare and Development (MSWDO) shall initiate the implementation of modules for the courses provided in sections (d) and (e) above in close coordination with CSOs with child-focused programs.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec29" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">2. Health Care</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 29. Primary Health Care. —<span class="section-top"> The Local Government Unit of Libacao shall implement primary health care and nutrition programs for children in coordination with the MHO, MSWDO, and DOH.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec30" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 30. Promotion of Primary Health Care Programs. —<span class="section-top"> Barangay Health Centers shall implement the Primary Health Care Program and designate a Barangay Child Health Officer to monitor children’s health at the barangay level.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b>To ensure the implementation of this section, the Local Government Unit of Libacao shall take appropriate measures in:</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a)</b> Combating disease and malnutrition within the framework of primary health care through, among other measures, providing clean water and nutritious food, taking into consideration the dangers and risks of environmental pollution, and applying readily available technology;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b)</b> Establishing a Parents Orientation Development Program which includes gender responsive courses on reproductive health, child health, and child-rearing practices in the context of the Filipino psychology;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c)</b> Monitoring the full implementation of the Milk Code of the Philippines (Executive Order No. 51) and The Rooming-In and Breastfeeding Act (RA 7600, as amended by RA 10028) and advocate for the prosecution of milk firms in violation of such code; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d)</b> Conducting massive information and education program about breastfeeding and ensuring its integration into the curricula of all schools.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec31" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 31. Child and Gender Sensitivity Training for Health Workers. —<span class="section-top"> All Barangay Health Workers (BHWs) shall attend training courses on child and gender sensitivity to be conducted by the MCPC.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec32" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 32. Child-and Family-Friendly Health Facilities. —<span class="section-top"> Health facilities shall set up child-friendly units that include rooming-in and pediatric-appropriate mechanisms and gadgets. The Municipal Government of Libacao shall adopt an <b>Adolescent Reproductive Health Program</b> for all Libacaonon adolescents, whether in or out of school. This program shall ensure proper knowledge management regarding reproductive health, teenage pregnancy, and risky sexual and non-sexual behaviors, among others.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec33" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 33. Mental Health Program. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipal Government of Libacao shall adopt a comprehensive mental health program that includes wide range of promotive, preventive, and rehabilitative treatment. This program shall be available to all individuals, especially children and young adults across all walks of life, and shall be integrated into various treatment settings from community to referral to facilities backed with institutional support mechanisms from different government agencies and CSOs. This shall employ peer-to-peer counseling, training for counselors, and the hiring/designation of a guidance counselor for each school in the municipality.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec34" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 34. Program for Children with Disabilities. —<span class="section-top"> The BCPCs shall conduct periodic child-specific comprehensive surveys on children with disabilities in their respective jurisdiction as a basis for a more systematic coordination of services (health, nutrition and education) for children with special needs, and for the purpose of consolidation at the municipal and provincial levels.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec35" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 35. Educators and Health Professionals Training for Special Needs. —<span class="section-top"> A training program for educators and health professionals handling children with disabilities and special needs shall be a priority of the Municipal Government of Libacao.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec36" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">3. Integrated Early Childhood Care and Development: The "Under Six" Framework. <span style="color:red">(c/o MSWDO/ DCW)</span></span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 36. Under Six Program Framework. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipality of Libacao shall institutionalize the "Under Six" Program as a comprehensive strategy to provide integrated health, nutrition, and early education services for children from birth to age six (6). This program shall serve as the primary vehicle for delivering holistic interventions during the child's most formative years. Survival and development programs shall conform with <b>RA 6972 (Barangay-Level Total Development and Protection of Children Act)</b> and the <b>ECCD Act (RA 10410)</b>.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec37" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 37. Setting Up of Child Development Centers by Barangay-Based Organizations. —<span class="section-top"> In compliance with the Early Childhood Care and Development (ECCD) Law, the Municipality shall ensure the establishment, maintenance, and continuous improvement of at least one (1) Day Care Center in every barangay. These centers shall serve as the frontline hubs for the "Under Six" Program and shall be designed to provide a safe, nurturing, and stimulating environment for young learners.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b>To complement these efforts, Barangay-Based Organizations are encouraged to mobilize resources and collaborate with the Municipal Government in establishing and supporting these centers. Through this partnership, the Municipality ensures that a greater number of children in need of developmental attention are reached and served within their respective communities.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec38" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 38. Child Development Worker and Support Group. —<span class="section-top"> To ensure the effective operation of the Day Care Centers within the "Under Six" Framework, the following support systems shall be established:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a)</b>	Personnel and Technical Support: The Municipal and Barangay governments shall provide for the Child Development Worker’s (CDW) allowance and salary. The Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO) shall provide continuing technical assistance, professional training, and administrative supervision to the centers.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b)</b>	Community Volunteer Parent Groups: Every Day Care Center shall organize a Community Volunteer Parent Group to effectively support the center. These groups shall mobilize local resources for the improvement of facilities, the acquisition of learning materials and play equipment, and other supportive activities that enhance the child's environment.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec39" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">4.Recreational and Cultural Programs</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 39. Barangay-Level Recreational and Cultural Facilities and Programs. —<span class="section-top"> Every barangay shall allocate space for age-appropriate recreational facilities. The Culture and Arts Development Council shall research, document, and revive indigenous games and pastimes, integrating them into municipal athletic programs and year-round cultural activities that respect cultural diversity.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec40" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 40. Local Children’s Literature. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipal Government of Libacao shall invest in the promotion and development of local children’s literature, as well as the establishment and support of public libraries and community learning hubs accessible to all Libacaonon children.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec41" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 41. Other Child-Friendly Facilities. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipal Government of Libacao, in partnership with CSOs and civic organizations, is encouraged to establish and maintain other child-friendly facilities, such as, but not limited to, museums, parks, playgrounds, and youth activity centers.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				
				<section id="art5-sec42" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">5. National Children’s Month</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 42. November as National Children’s Month. —<span class="section-top"> Pursuant to Republic Act No. 10661, the Municipal Council for the Protection of Children (MCPC) and all Barangay Councils for the Protection of Children (BCPCs) shall lead the annual observance of National Children’s Month every November through child-focused activities promoting the rights, responsibilities, and welfare of children.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec43" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">6. Crisis Intervention Program</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 43. Crisis Intervention Center. —<span class="section-top"> Pursuant to Republic Act No. 7610 and Republic Act No. 8505, the Municipal Government of Libacao shall establish a Crisis Intervention Center to provide temporary shelter, protective care, psychosocial support, and referral services for abused children and women in crisis. To ensure safety and privacy, separate and distinct facilities shall be provided for male and female child clients.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				
				<section id="art5-sec44" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 44. Objectives of the Crisis Intervention Center. —<span class="section-top"> The Crisis Intervention Center shall pursue the following objectives:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Protective Care:</b> Provide safe, temporary shelter and basic necessities to abused children and women in crisis;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Multidisciplinary Healing:</b> Network with government agencies and civil society organizations for medical, psychological, and legal services to restore the physical and emotional well-being of clients;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Access to Justice:</b> Empower and support victims in pursuing legal remedies and participating in court proceedings when ready;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Therapeutic &amp; Livelihood Support:</b> Offer life skills, vocational training, and post-shelter community reintegration assistance to promote independence;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e) Public Advocacy:</b> Conduct municipal-wide educational campaigns to raise awareness on preventing child abuse and domestic violence while promoting the rights of women and children; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>f) Policy Coordination:</b> Partner with provincial and national entities to strengthen law enforcement and policy frameworks for the protection of vulnerable sectors.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec45" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 45. Organizational Structure, Management, and Operations. —<span class="section-top"> The management and operational framework of the Crisis Intervention Center shall be governed by the following guidelines:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Administrative Management:</b> The Center shall operate under the direct management of the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO), with technical, logistical, and operational support from the Provincial Social Welfare and Development Office (PSWDO);</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Organizational Structure:</b> The Municipal Council for the Protection of Children (MCPC) shall formulate and periodically review the organizational structure and staffing pattern of the Center based on service demands;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Multi-Sectoral Support:</b> Member agencies of the MCPC, national government agencies, and CSOs pledging support shall formalize their commitments through a Memorandum of Agreement (MOA) to ensure program sustainability; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Operational Guidelines:</b> The MSWDO, in coordination with the MCPC, shall issue the necessary rules, regulations, and policies for the effective operation and administration of the Center.</p>
				</section>

				
				<section id="art5-sec46" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 46. Holding Area and Facility for Children at Risk (CAR) and Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL). —<span class="section-top"> Pursuant to Republic Act No. 9344, as amended, the Municipal Government of Libacao shall establish or designate a twenty-four-hour (24-hour) holding area and temporary facility for Children at Risk (CAR) and Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL). The facility shall provide temporary protective care, supervision, and basic necessities for CICL awaiting court disposition, diversion proceedings, or transfer to appropriate regional facilities.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b>The Municipal Council for the Protection of Children (MCPC), in coordination with the MSWDO and local law enforcement agencies, shall formulate standard operating protocols governing the proper intake, custody, diversion, and referral of CAR and CICL.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art5-sec47" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 47. Budget Allocation for Operations and Maintenance. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipal Government of Libacao shall annually appropriate the necessary funds for the continuous operation, maintenance, and administrative support of the Crisis Intervention Center and the holding facility for Children at Risk (CAR) and Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL). These funds shall form an integral part of the annual operating budget of the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO).</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				
				<section id="art-sec" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

