			<article id="art6" class="article-card">
				<h1 class="articleNumber"><b>ARTICLE 6</b></h1>
				<h1 class="articleTitle">SPECIAL CONCERNS AND EMERGING ISSUES</h1>
				
				<section id="art6-sec48" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">A. Prohibited Acts</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 48. Prohibited Acts. —<span class="section-top"> In addition to all acts defined and penalized under Republic Act No. 7610 (Child Abuse and Exploitation Act), Republic Act No. 7658 (Child Labor Act), Republic Act No. 9208 (Anti-Trafficking in Persons Act), Republic Act No. 9262 (Anti-VAWC Act), Republic Act No. 11313 (Safe Spaces Act), Republic Act No. 10627 (Anti-Bullying Act), and other pertinent national laws, the following acts shall likewise be prohibited and punishable within the Municipality of Libacao:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a)	Human Trafficking:</b> Acts of trafficking in persons as defined under Section 4 of Republic Act No. 9208, as amended;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Peonage of Children:</b> Offering or subjecting the services of a child as payment for debt or in exchange for a favor, including but not limited to forced labor in agricultural plantations or domestic servitude as house-helpers;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Pre-Arrangement for Marriage:</b> Pre-arrangements or formal negotiations made by parents, guardians, or relatives to marry off a child or ward;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Commercial Exploitation in Benefit Dances:</b> Utilizing or facilitating the engagement of children as dancing partners for payment or fees during benefit dances or social gatherings;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e) Discrimination Against Girl Children:</b> Depriving, restricting, or prohibiting girl children from accessing formal education;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>f) Discrimination Against Illegitimate Children:</b> Refusal by educational institutions to enroll a child, or requiring a parental marriage contract as a prerequisite for school admission;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>g) Pregnancy-Based Academic Penalties:</b> Imposing penalties of expulsion, refusal of graduation, or withholding of school clearances against a student by reason of pregnancy, provided academic requirements are met;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>h) Physical and Degrading Punishment:</b> Subjecting a child to corporal or degrading punishment, including but not limited to kneeling on salt, hanging, head-shaving, stripping clothes, locking in enclosed spaces (aparadors), hair-pulling, or exposure to extreme heat or insects;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>i) Forced Live-In Arrangements:</b> Enticing, encouraging, or forcing a child to cohabitate with any person as husband and wife in exchange for money, property, or consideration;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>j) Sexual Exploitation:</b> Consorting with unrelated children or engaging in public displays of lascivious conduct in the presence of minors;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>k) Pornography and Indecent Shows:</b> Producing, transporting, selling, or distributing pornographic materials, or utilizing children in indecent shows in public or private venues;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>l) Restricted Substance Exposure:</b> Selling, distributing, or giving liquor, cigarettes, vaping products, e-cigarettes, rugby, or addictive substances to a child, or utilizing a child to purchase the same;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>m) Public Safety and Minor Conduct Violations:</b> Permitting minors to drive motor vehicles without a valid driver's license, overloading minors in public or private conveyances, smoking/vaping in enclosed public spaces, or violating established curfew, loitering, and anti-vandalism ordinances;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>n) Statutory Rape (RA 11648):</b> Engaging in sexual acts with a child sixteen (16) years of age or below. Pursuant to Republic Act No. 11648, any sexual act committed against a person below sixteen (16) years of age shall be considered rape, as a child of such age is legally deemed incapable of giving valid consent.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b><i>Close-in-Age Exception:</i> Provided, that the act shall not be considered statutory rape if:</p>
					<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>•	Both parties are minors;</p>
					<p class="section-inner2">•	The age difference between them is not more than three (3) years; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner2">•	The sexual act was consensual, non-abusive, and non-exploitative.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>o) Harmful Foods in School Premises:</b> Selling or distributing junk food, artificially sweetened snacks, or carbonated beverages within school canteens and immediate school premises; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>p) Gender-Based Sexual Harassment (RA 11313):</b> Committing acts of street, public space, or online sexual harassment against children pursuant to the Safe Spaces Act.</p>
				</b></section><b>
				
				<section id="art6-sec49" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">B. Control on Exposure to New Technology</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 49. Control on Children’s Exposure to Commercial Video Games and Internet Gaming. —<span class="section-top"> To prioritize the education, health, and well-being of the youth, commercial establishments renting out electronic video games or providing internet, computer, and digital gaming access to children shall be subject to the following restrictions:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Prohibited Operating Hours for Minors:</b> Access by children shall be strictly prohibited during the following hours:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>1.	<i>School Days:</i> From 7:00 AM to 11:00 AM and from 1:00 PM to 4:00 PM; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>2.	<i>Evening Hours (All Days):</i> Beyond 7:00 PM on any day of the week.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Administrative Penalties:</b> Any business establishment or operator found in violation of these prescribed hours shall be subject to the following administrative sanctions:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>1.	<i>First Offense:</i> Suspension of Business Permit and License to Operate for fifteen (15) days;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>2.	<i>Second Offense:</i> Suspension of Business Permit and License to Operate for one (1) month; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>3.	<i>Third Offense:</i> Revocation/Cancellation of Business Permit and permanent closure of the establishment.</p>
					<p class="section-p"><b></b>Implementation and enforcement of this section shall be executed in coordination with the Business Permits and Licensing Office (BPLO) and local law enforcement agencies, in accordance with existing municipal licensing and tax ordinances.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec50" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 50. Control on Children’s Exposure to Internet Cafés. —<span class="section-top"> Internet cafés, and computer rental shops catering to children during school days from 7:00 AM to 11:00 AM and from 1:00 PM to 4:00 PM, as well as beyond 7:00 PM on all days, shall be subject to the following regulations and administrative sanctions:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Administrative Penalties:</b> Any business establishment found catering to children during restricted hours without valid cause shall face the following penalties:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>1.	<i>First Offense:</i> Suspension of Business Permit and License to Operate for fifteen (15) days;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>2.	<i>Second Offense:</i> Suspension of Business Permit and License to Operate for one (1) month; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>3.	<i>Third Offense:</i> Revocation/Cancellation of Business Permit and permanent closure of the establishment.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Exceptions for Educational and Permitted Access:</b> The presence of a child in an internet café during restricted hours shall not constitute a violation if:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>1.	<i>Academic/Research Use:</i> The child is conducting school-related research, completing educational assignments, or attending online classes, as verified by a valid school ID, teacher's certification, or official school assignment module; or</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b>2.	<i>Parental Guidance:</i> The child is physically accompanied by, or presents written authorization with a contact number from, a parent or legal guardian.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Security Restrictions and Content Filtering:</b> All commercial establishments providing internet and computer access referred to in this section and Section 49 must install and activate parental control software, firewall restrictions, and content filters on every computer terminal to block access to pornographic, gambling, violent, or harmful websites. Failure to maintain these technical safeguards shall be penalized in accordance with existing national laws or the penalty provisions under this Code.</p>
				</section>
				
				
				
				<section id="art6-sec51" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 51. Regulation of Children’s Exposure to Amusement Centers. —<span class="section-top"> An Amusement Center or Place of Recreation is defined as any establishment offering entertainment facilities such as, but not limited to, billiards, pool, karaoke, dance machines, and computer games. To ensure the safety and moral well-being of minors:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Parental Supervision:</b> Children shall be strictly prohibited from entering such amusement centers unless accompanied by a parent or legal guardian.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Prohibition on Games of Chance:</b> Children are strictly prohibited from participating in or being in the immediate vicinity of games of chance within these establishments.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Mandatory Orientation for Business Owners:</b> All commercial establishments referred to in Sections 49, 50, and 51 shall be required to undergo an official orientation on the Safe and Responsible Use of New Technologies and the provisions of this Code. Successful completion of this orientation shall be a prerequisite for the issuance or renewal of Business Permits.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec52" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">C. Children in Situations of Armed Conflict, Trafficking, and Domestic Violence</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 52. Protection for Children in Especially Difficult Circumstances. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipal Government of Libacao shall ensure that children facing extreme vulnerabilities and difficult circumstances are afforded full legal, social, and humanitarian protection:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Children in Situations of Armed Conflict (CSAC):</b> Children affected by armed conflict shall be considered victims and not combatants under any circumstances. They shall be afforded full protection, relief, rehabilitation, and reintegration services in accordance with Article X of Republic Act No. 7610 and Republic Act No. 11188 (Special Protection of Children in Situations of Armed Conflict Act). The Municipality shall ensure that schools, day care centers, healthcare facilities, and child-priority spaces are strictly declared and maintained as "Zones of Peace."</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Victims of Trafficking:</b> Children who are victims of human trafficking shall be afforded full protection, emergency assistance, and legal support in accordance with Section 16 of Republic Act No. 9208 (Anti-Trafficking in Persons Act of 2003), as amended. The Municipality hereby adopts the Philippine Guidelines for the Protection of Children (PGPGC) to guarantee that the rescue, recovery, return, and reintegration of child-victims are handled with gender sensitivity and the paramount consideration of the child's best interest.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Victims of Domestic Violence:</b> Children who are direct victims, witnesses, or co-victims of domestic abuse shall be provided immediate protective custody, temporary shelter, legal assistance, and psychosocial support in accordance with Republic Act No. 9262 (Anti-Violence Against Women and Their Children Act of 2004).</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec53" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">D. Children of Indigenous Peoples</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 53. Rights of Children of Indigenous Peoples. —<span class="section-top"> In addition to the rights guaranteed under this Code, Republic Act No. 8371 (The Indigenous Peoples' Rights Act of 1997), and other pertinent national laws, children of Indigenous Peoples (IP) residing or present within the Municipality of Libacao shall be entitled to the following protections:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Cultural Integrity:</b> Children of indigenous peoples shall be entitled to protection, survival, development, and participation rights that are consistent with the customs, traditions, and cultural values of their respective communities.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Culture-Specific Education:</b> The Municipal Council for the Protection of Children (MCPC), in coordination with the Department of Education (DepEd) and the National Commission on Indigenous Peoples (NCIP), shall develop and institute alternative systems of learning and Indigenous Peoples Education (IPEd) programs that are culture-specific, linguistically appropriate, and relevant to the needs of IP children.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Prioritization of Basic Social Services:</b> The delivery of basic social services in health, sanitation, and nutrition to children of indigenous cultural communities shall be prioritized:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	Hospitals, rural health units (RHUs), and barangay health stations shall ensure equal access, immediate attention, and non-discriminatory treatment for IP children; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	In the provision of healthcare and nutrition services, traditional and indigenous health practices shall be respected, recognized, and integrated where safe and appropriate.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Non-Discrimination and Equal Protection:</b> Children of indigenous peoples shall not be subjected to any form of discrimination, stigmatization, ridicule, or exclusion in schools, healthcare facilities, sports activities, or any public or private space within the Municipality.</p>
				</section>
				
				<section id="art6-sec54" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">E. Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL)</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 54. Rights of a Child in Conflict with the Law. —<span class="section-top"> Pursuant to the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child (UN CRC) and Republic Act No. 9344 (Juvenile Justice and Welfare Act of 2006), as amended by Republic Act No. 10630, every child alleged, accused of, or adjudged as having infringed the penal law shall be afforded the following fundamental guarantees:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Presumption of Innocence:</b> To be presumed innocent until proven guilty according to law.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Right to Information and Legal Assistance:</b> To be informed promptly and directly of the charges, through their parents or legal guardians when appropriate, and to have legal or other appropriate assistance in the preparation and presentation of a defense.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Prompt and Impartial Adjudication:</b> To have the matter determined without delay by a competent, independent, and impartial authority or judicial body in a fair hearing. This shall take place in the presence of legal counsel and the child’s parents or guardians, unless deemed contrary to the best interest of the child.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Protection Against Self-Incrimination:</b> Not to be compelled to give testimony or to confess guilt.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e) Right to Confront Witnesses:</b> To examine adverse witnesses and to obtain the participation and examination of witnesses on their behalf under conditions of equality.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>f) Right to Appeal:</b> To have any decision or measure imposed reviewed by a higher competent and impartial authority or judicial body according to law.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>g) Right to an Interpreter:</b> To have the free assistance of an interpreter if the child cannot understand or speak the language used in the proceedings.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>h) Right to Privacy and Confidentiality:</b> To have their privacy fully respected at all stages of the proceedings. This includes the non-disclosure of records to the public, the protection of the child's identity from media exposure, and the strict maintenance of confidential law enforcement and social work records pursuant to Section 43 of RA 9344.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec55" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 55. System of Diversion. —<span class="section-top"> A System of Diversion is hereby established within the Municipality, wherein Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL) shall, as much as possible, be referred to alternative, child-appropriate measures without undergoing formal court proceedings, pursuant to Section 23 of Republic Act No. 9344 (Juvenile Justice and Welfare Act of 2006), as amended by Republic Act No. 10630.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Applicability and Jurisdiction:</b> Diversion shall apply to a child or youth as defined in Section 7 of this Code who is alleged to have committed an offense, subject to the conditions, age thresholds, and penalty limits set forth under national law. For offenses punishable by imprisonment of not more than six (6) years, diversion proceedings shall be conducted at the Barangay or Law Enforcement level prior to any formal court filing.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Procedural Framework:</b> The handling of CICL cases and the conduct of diversion proceedings shall strictly adhere to the Barangay Protocol in Managing Cases of Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL) and Children at Risk (CAR) as issued, updated, and promulgated by the Juvenile Justice and Welfare Council (JJWC).</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Localized Diversion Programs:</b> The Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO), in coordination with the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC), shall formulate, supervise, and monitor localized diversion programs. Depending on the nature of the offense and the best interest of the child, diversion contracts may include, but are not limited to:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	Restitution of property or reparation of the damage caused;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	Written or oral apology to the victim and/or the community;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	Mandatory counseling for the child and their parent(s) or legal guardian(s);</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">4.	Attendance in life-skills trainings, seminars, and educational lectures;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">5.	Guidance and supervision by local social workers or designated community mentors; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">6.	Regulated community service tailored to the child's age, physical capacity, and school schedule.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec56" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 56. Stages Where Diversion May Be Conducted. —<span class="section-top"> In accordance with Section 24 of Republic Act No. 9344 (Juvenile Justice and Welfare Act of 2006), as amended by Republic Act No. 10630, diversion may be initiated and conducted at the following levels and phases of the proceedings:</span></h2>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>a) At the Barangay Level:</b> Through the Katarungang Pambarangay (Barangay Justice System), facilitated by the Lupon Tagapamayapa in coordination with the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC);</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>b) At the Law Enforcement Level:</b> During the police investigation stage, immediately following initial contact with the child and prior to formal referral to the prosecutor;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>c) At the Prosecution Level:</b> During the inquest or preliminary investigation stage conducted by the Municipal Circuit Trial Court or Provincial Prosecutor's Office; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>d) At the Judicial Level:</b> At any phase of the court proceedings before the formal entry of a judgment by the competent Family Court.</p>
					<p class="section-p">At all stages, the primary objective of diversion shall be to provide the child with an effective, community-based alternative to the formal criminal justice system, focusing on restorative justice, accountability, and the best interests of the child.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec57" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 57. Duty to Inform the Child of the Offense. —<span class="section-top"> All competent authorities, immediately upon initial contact with or taking custody of a child, are duty-bound to explain the reason for such contact and the legal consequences of the child’s alleged acts or omissions. This explanation must be conducted in a language, dialect, or manner directly known to and understood by the child.</span></h2>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Rehabilitative Intent:</b> The explanation of the child’s responsibility for the commission of any offense shall be conducted strictly with the primary objective of guidance, counseling, and social rehabilitation, rather than punitive interrogation.</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Inadmissibility of Statements:</b> In accordance with Section 44 of Republic Act No. 9344 and the constitutional right against self-incrimination, any statement, information, admission, or confession obtained from the child during initial contact, intake, diversion, or counseling sessions shall be treated as strictly confidential. Such statements shall be inadmissible as evidence for or against the child in any court of justice, quasi-judicial body, or administrative proceeding.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec58" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 58. Indigenous Modes of Diversion. —<span class="section-top"> In recognition of local heritage, traditional leadership, and the rights of Indigenous Cultural Communities (ICCs), indigenous modes of conflict resolution and customary justice systems shall be encouraged and utilized for children in conflict with the law or children at risk belonging to indigenous communities, pursuant to Section 13 of Republic Act No. 9344 and Republic Act No. 8371 (<i>The Indigenous Peoples' Rights Act of 1997</i>), provided they are consistent with national child rights standards.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Active Participation:</b> The active participation of the child, their family, and the council of elders or traditional leaders in efforts toward restorative justice and reconciliation shall be prioritized to achieve community-based healing and social harmony.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Parental Care and Responsibility:</b> The family shall exercise diligent parental care and supervision over the child. Parents or legal guardians shall be held civilly and administratively responsible for any negligence or failure to exercise parental authority, in accordance with the Family Code of the Philippines, the Civil Code, and pertinent provisions of Republic Act No. 9344, as amended.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Human Rights and Child Safeguards:</b> No indigenous mode of diversion or customary conflict resolution shall be utilized if it involves corporal punishment, cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment, forced arrangements, or any practice that violates the fundamental rights and best interests of the child as guaranteed under this Code, national laws, and the Philippine Constitution.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec59" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 59. Inadmissibility of Admissions and Rules on Voluntary Confessions. —<span class="section-top"> To preserve the integrity of the restorative justice process and uphold the child's constitutional right against self-incrimination, the following rules regarding admissions and confessions shall strictly apply within the Municipality:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Absolute Inadmissibility:</b> Any statement, admission, or confession made by a child during any stage of the diversion proceedings—whether at the barangay, law enforcement, prosecution, or judicial level—shall be strictly confidential and inadmissible as evidence against the child. This protection remains absolute even if the diversion proceedings fail or if the case proceeds to formal trial before the court, pursuant to Section 44 of Republic Act No. 9344, as amended.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Protection from Collateral Consequences:</b> No admission or confession made by a child shall be used to deny them fundamental rights, educational opportunities, or public privileges, nor shall it serve as a basis for discrimination, social stigmatization, or the imposition of any extrajudicial penalty outside the agreed diversion contract.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Formalities for Voluntary Confession:</b> If a child voluntarily admits or confesses to the commission of an offense, competent authorities shall determine the appropriate localized diversion program. To guarantee that such admission is executed freely, intelligently, and voluntarily:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	The statement must be reduced to writing in a clear language or dialect directly known to and understood by the child;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	The statement must be signed by the child and countersigned by their parent(s) or legal guardian(s); and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	The statement must be witnessed and signed by the Local Social Welfare and Development Officer (LSWDO), legal counsel, or in their absence, at least one (1) of the following: a relative, a member of an accredited child-focused non-governmental organization, a religious representative, or a member of the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC), who shall certify that the confession was executed voluntarily and without coercion.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Non-Admission and Guidance:</b> If the child does not admit or confess to the offense, no force, threat, intimidation, or undue influence shall be exerted to induce an admission. Where the weight of evidence points to the child's involvement, competent authorities shall guide the child toward realizing their responsibility and obligations through restorative counseling, focusing on accountability and community healing rather than punitive pressure.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec60" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 60. Termination of Case. —<span class="section-top"> If, at any stage of the proceedings before the barangay, law enforcement, prosecution, or court, the competent authorities determine that the child did not commit the alleged offense, or that there is no probable cause to proceed, the case shall be immediately terminated and dismissed.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Written Decision and Official Record Closure:</b> A formal written decision or resolution of dismissal shall be issued, officially closing the matter in the registries and case files of the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC), the Philippine National Police (PNP) Women and Children Protection Desk, and the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO).</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Duty to Explain and Restore Reputation:</b> The competent authorities are required to explain the decision clearly to both the child (and their parent/guardian) and the complainant in a language or dialect known to and understood by them. Upon request of the child or their parents, a formal Certificate of Case Dismissal shall be issued to affirmatively clear the child's reputation in school and community records.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Expungement and Sealing of Records:</b> Upon termination of the case under this Section, any fingerprints, photographs, temporary intake notes, blotter entries, or case records created during the investigation shall be sealed, expunged, or destroyed in accordance with Section 43 of Republic Act No. 9344, as amended, ensuring that no prior accusation hinders the child's future educational, employment, or civil opportunities.</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b></p><b>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b></b></p><b>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p><b>
				</b></b></b></section><b><b><b>

				<section id="art6-sec61" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 61. Prohibition Against Labeling and Shaming. —<span class="section-top"> From the moment of initial contact and throughout all stages of the proceedings, all competent authorities, law enforcement officers, social welfare officers, and local officials are strictly prohibited from branding, labeling, or treating a child in conflict with the law or a child at risk in a derogatory or discriminatory manner, pursuant to Section 19 of Republic Act No. 9344, as amended.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Prohibited Designations:</b> Authorities must refrain from labeling or referring to minors using derogatory terms, including but not limited to "young criminals," "juvenile delinquents," "prostitutes," "snatchers," "rugby boys," or any other stigmatizing designation, whether spoken, written, or implied in official intake reports.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Non-Discrimination:</b> No discriminatory remarks, biases, or unequal practices shall be tolerated, particularly regarding the child’s social class, economic status, gender, religious beliefs, or ethnic and indigenous origins.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Prohibition on Public Disclosure and Social Media Shaming:</b> To prevent public shaming and lifelong stigmatization, the identity, photographs, mugshots, address, or personal circumstances of a child shall not be published in print or broadcast media, nor posted, shared, or live-streamed on any social media platforms or public bulletin boards by any responding officer, barangay official, or municipal employee, in accordance with Section 43 of Republic Act No. 9344.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Administrative and Legal Liability:</b> Any officer, employee, or official of the Municipal or Barangay Government found violating any provision of this Section shall be subjected to administrative disciplinary proceedings under Republic Act No. 6713 (Code of Conduct and Ethical Standards for Public Officials and Employees) and Civil Service laws, without prejudice to civil damages or criminal liability under Republic Act No. 9344 and other applicable laws.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec62" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 62. Prohibited Acts Against Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL). —<span class="section-top"> Any and all acts, practices, or omissions prejudicial or detrimental to the physical, psychological, emotional, moral, spiritual, or social well-being of a child in conflict with the law or a child at risk are strictly prohibited within the Municipality.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Coercive and Abusive Measures:</b> Authorities, law enforcement officers, barangay officials, and custodians shall refrain from employing threats, intimidation, coercion, or abusive practices. Prohibited acts—whether committed directly or indirectly by persons in authority or those acting on their behalf—include, but are not limited to:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	Cursing, using profane language, or uttering verbally abusive statements;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	Beating, slapping, or any form of physical assault or corporal punishment;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	Stripping the child of their clothing or subjecting them to forced exposure; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">4.	Detaining minors in standard police cells, lockup facilities, or together with adult offenders, in direct violation of statutory segregation mandates under national law.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Degrading, Inhumane, and Cruel Punishments:</b> In accordance with the Constitution and Republic Act No. 9745 (Anti-Torture Act of 2009), the following cruel, inhuman, or degrading acts are strictly prohibited under all circumstances:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	Shaving the heads of minors or altering their physical appearance as a disciplinary measure;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	Pouring irritating, corrosive, or harmful substances over their bodies; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	Forcing minors to wear signs, garments, or perform public acts intended to embarrass, humiliate, or degrade their dignity before the community.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Involuntary Servitude and Forced Labor:</b> No child shall be subjected to involuntary servitude or forced labor in any form. It is strictly prohibited for arresting officers, custodians, social workers, or barangay personnel to compel minors to act as errand runners, cleaners, domestic helpers, or to perform any labor for the personal or official benefit of authorities.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Legal Liabilities and Penalties:</b> Any person in authority, law enforcement officer, or public employee who commits, permits, or tolerates any of the prohibited acts enumerated in this Section shall be prosecuted criminally and held administratively liable under Sections 60 and 61 of Republic Act No. 9344, Republic Act No. 7610, Republic Act No. 9745, Article 235 of the Revised Penal Code, and applicable Civil Service laws, without prejudice to civil liability for damages.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec63" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 63. Diversion Programs; Criteria and Implementation. —<span class="section-top"> Once competent authorities and participating parties determine that a child in conflict with the law (CICL) shall undergo diversion, the formulation, execution, and monitoring of the diversion program shall be strictly governed by the provisions of Republic Act No. 9344 (The Juvenile Justice and Welfare Act of 2006), as amended.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Individualized Basis:</b> The diversion program shall be customized to the specific needs of the child, taking into consideration their age, level of maturity, gravity of the offense, degree of responsibility, and surrounding family and socioeconomic circumstances.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Restorative Objectives:</b> The program shall adhere strictly to the principles of restorative justice, aiming to repair the harm caused to the victim and the community, facilitate reconciliation, and equip the child with practical life skills, values formation, and guidance counseling to prevent recidivism.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Jurisdictional Levels and Components:</b> In accordance with Sections 23, 24, and 31 of Republic Act No. 9344, diversion programs shall be executed across three distinct operational tiers:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	<i>Barangay Level (Offenses with imposable penalty of 6 years or less):</i> Facilitated by the Punong Barangay in coordination with the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC) and the Local Social Welfare and Development Office (LSWDO). Interventions may include restitution, reparation of damages, written or oral apology, community service, and mandatory guidance counseling;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	<i>Law Enforcement and Prosecution Level (Offenses with imposable penalty exceeding 6 years but not more than 12 years):</i> Formulated by the investigating law enforcement officer or prosecutor in coordination with the LSWDO. Interventions may include structured counseling, anger management or values formation seminars, periodic reporting to a social worker, and family support programs; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	<i>Judicial Level:</i> Diversion programs ordered directly by the Family Court or competent court in accordance with specialized judicial rules for juveniles and court-supervised diversion contracts.&gt;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Supervision and Monitoring:</b> The Local Social Welfare and Development Officer (LSWDO), in coordination with the BCPC, shall monitor the child’s progress and compliance with the Contract of Diversion. Failure to comply with the terms of the diversion program without justifiable cause shall result in the termination of diversion and the continuation of formal legal proceedings in accordance with national law.</p>
				</section>

