				<section id="art6-sec64" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 64. Factors in Determining the Diversion Program. —<span class="section-top"> In determining whether diversion is appropriate for a child in conflict with the law (CICL) and in designing a customized diversion plan, the competent authorities, in coordination with the Local Social Welfare and Development Office (LSWDO), shall evaluate the factors set forth under Section 29 of Republic Act No. 9344, including:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Nature and Gravity of the Offense:</b> Evaluating the specific circumstances of the act, the extent of physical or material harm caused to the victim, and its overall impact on the community;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Socio-Cultural and Family Circumstances:</b> Assessing the child's home environment, family stability, educational status, and economic conditions;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Personal and Developmental Factors:</b> Considering the child's age, level of emotional and psychological maturity, state of mind, and the presence of peer pressure or elder influence;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Record of Prior Offenses:</b> Determining whether the child is a first-time offender or has a documented history of prior contact with law enforcement or local authorities;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e) Availability of Local Support Systems:</b> Assessing the capacity and commitment of parents or guardians to provide adequate supervision, alongside the availability of community-based intervention programs within the Municipality; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>f) Restorative Potential:</b> Evaluating the child's willingness to acknowledge responsibility for their conduct, express remorse, and participate in good faith in repairing the harm caused to the victim and the community.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec65" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 65. Criteria for Formulating Diversion Programs. —<span class="section-top"> In formulating a diversion program, the individual characteristics and peculiar circumstances of the child shall be the primary basis for a personalized and individualized treatment plan. In accordance with Section 31 of Republic Act No. 9344, the following factors shall be considered:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Remorse of the Child:</b> The child’s feelings of remorse and their willingness to acknowledge and take responsibility for the offense committed;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Parental Supervision:</b> The capacity, willingness, and commitment of the parents or legal guardians to provide adequate guidance and supervision throughout the duration of the program;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Victim’s Perspective:</b> The views, concerns, and interests of the victim regarding the propriety and adequacy of the proposed measures, ensuring a restorative and balanced outcome;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Community Resources:</b> The availability and accessibility of community-based programs within the Municipality or Barangay for the rehabilitation, re-education, and social reintegration of the child; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e) Developmental Impact and Protection:</b> The potential of the program to foster the child's positive development and prevent reoffending, ensuring that interventions are non-stigmatizing, age-appropriate, and do not cause further trauma.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec66" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 66. Components of the Diversion Program. —<span class="section-top"> The diversion program shall include adequate socio-cultural and psychological responses and services tailored to the child's developmental needs. Pursuant to Section 31 of Republic Act No. 9344, as amended, diversion measures may include, but are not limited to, the following:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Restorative Justice Measures:</b></p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	Written or oral apology to the victim and the community;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	Restitution of property;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	Reparation of the damage caused;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">4.	Indemnification for consequential damages; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">5.	Confiscation and forfeiture of the proceeds or instruments of the crime, or fine where permitted by law;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Guidance and Counseling Services:</b></p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	Formal written or oral reprimand or citation;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	Guidance and supervision orders; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	Mandatory structured counseling for both the child and their parent(s) or legal guardian(s);</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Skills Development and Education:</b></p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	Trainings, seminars, and workshops on anger management, problem-solving, conflict resolution, values formation, and life skills designed to prevent re-offending; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	Participation in formal, non-formal, or vocational education and life-skills training programs; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Community and Institutional Support:</b></p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	Active participation in community-based programs, including youth leadership, sports, environmental, or community service activities; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	Institutional care and custody, utilized strictly as a measure of last resort and for the shortest appropriate period of time, in an accredited <i>Bahay Pag-asa</i> or Youth Care Facility specifically designed for children in conflict with the law.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec67" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 67. Criteria for Community-Based Programs. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipality of Libacao shall establish, fund, and maintain community-based programs focused on the prevention, holistic rehabilitation, and social reintegration of Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL) and Children at Risk (CAR). All such programs shall meet the following criteria:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Primary Objective:</b> The fundamental purpose of the program must be the holistic rehabilitation, development, and successful social reintegration of the child into their family and community;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Informed Consent:</b> Referral to and participation in these programs shall strictly require the voluntary and informed consent of the child and their parent(s) or legal guardian(s);</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Multi-Sectoral Collaboration:</b> The active participation of child-centered agencies, religious institutions, civic organizations, non-governmental organizations (NGOs), and community stakeholders shall be encouraged to expand the support and mentoring network available to the child;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Youth Organization Involvement:</b> Programs shall actively facilitate the constructive inclusion of the child in recognized youth organizations, including:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	The Sangguniang Kabataan (SK);</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	The Pag-Asa Youth Association of the Philippines, Inc. (PYAP); and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	Other accredited community-based youth organizations that foster values formation, leadership, and total development; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e) Community-Based Accessibility and Non-Disruption:</b> Programs must be anchored within the barangay or municipal community to ensure the child remains in their natural social environment, thereby avoiding unnecessary institutionalization and minimizing disruption to their education, vocational training, and family life.</p>
				</b></section><b>

				<section id="art6-sec68" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 68. Community Reintegration Programs. —<span class="section-top"> To ensure the successful transition of a child in conflict with the law (CICL) or a child at risk (CAR) back into mainstream society and prevent recidivism, the Local Social Welfare and Development Office (LSWDO/MSWDO), in coordination with the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC), shall establish and maintain structured community reintegration programs. Pursuant to Section 51 of Republic Act No. 9344, as amended, these programs shall include, but are not limited to, the following:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Community Service:</b> Engaging the child in meaningful, age-appropriate, non-hazardous, and non-exploitative community service projects designed to foster civic responsibility and positive social contribution. These activities may include:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	Participation in local environmental protection, tree planting, or community sanitation drives;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	Assistance in barangay or municipal socio-cultural and sports events; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	Volunteer work for local social welfare initiatives under the direct supervision of the LSWDO or BCPC;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Organizational Membership and Social Networking:</b> Facilitating the child’s membership and active participation in recognized civic, religious, and youth organizations to provide a supportive, positive peer environment and moral guidance;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Educational and Vocational Re-Integration:</b> Assisting the child in re-enrolling in the formal education system, or facilitating enrollment in the Alternative Learning System (ALS) or Technical Education and Skills Development Authority (TESDA)-accredited vocational training courses to enhance functional literacy and economic self-sufficiency; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Peer Mentorship and Guidance:</b> Partnering the child with trained youth mentors from the Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) or accredited community youth organizations to provide continuous peer guidance, moral support, and positive role modeling during the reintegration process.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec69" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 69. Conditions for the Diversion Program. —<span class="section-top"> In all cases where a child in conflict with the law (CICL) is required to undergo a diversion program, the following mandatory conditions shall be incorporated into the formal Contract of Diversion:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Execution of the Contract:</b> A written Contract of Diversion—specifying the localized intervention program, duration, and individual obligations—shall be signed by the child, their parent(s) or legal guardian(s), the Local Social Welfare and Development Officer (LSWDO), and the competent authority concerned (e.g., Punong Barangay, Law Enforcement Officer, or Prosecutor);</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Periodic Reporting:</b> The child shall report to the assigned social worker or designated authority at least once a month for a formal progress evaluation and review of the program’s effectiveness;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Compliance and Good Faith:</b> The child shall cooperate fully and faithfully comply with all terms, schedules, and conditions stipulated in the signed contract;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Home Visitation and Monitoring:</b> The child and their family shall permit the LSWDO or authorized representatives of the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC) to conduct periodic home visits to monitor the child’s living environment and reintegration progress. All visits shall be conducted during reasonable hours and with due respect for the family's constitutional rights to privacy and dignity; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e) Separability and Validity of Additional Conditions:</b> The parties may agree to additional lawful conditions relevant to the child's rehabilitation, provided they are not contrary to law, morals, good customs, public order, or public policy. Should any specific condition be declared invalid or void, it shall be severable and shall not affect the enforceability of the remaining conditions in the contract.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec70" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 70. Assistance of the Child by Parents or Advocates. —<span class="section-top"> To ensure the validity of proceedings, safeguard the best interests of the child, and protect their constitutional rights, any agreement or contract resulting from a Barangay conciliation or diversion process under Section 23 of Republic Act No. 9344 shall be executed by the child with the formal assistance and countersignature of a responsible adult.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Order of Preference for Assisting Adults:</b> The contract or conciliation agreement shall be signed by the child and countersigned by any of the following persons, in order of preference:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	The parent(s) or legal guardian;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	The Local Social Welfare and Development Officer (LSWDO) or designated social worker;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	A relative of the child within the fourth degree of consanguinity or affinity;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">4.	An accredited member of a child-focused faith-based organization; or</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">5.	A designated member of the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC) concerned.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Legal Effect of Non-Compliance:</b> Any conciliation agreement or diversion contract executed without the formal assistance and signature of one of the authorized assisting parties listed above shall be null and void ab initio and without any legal or binding effect.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Duty of the Assisting Party:</b> The assisting adult shall have the explicit duty to explain the terms, consequences, and obligations of the agreement to the child in a language and manner understood by the child. They shall verify and ensure that the child's consent is given voluntarily, knowingly, and free from any form of coercion, threat, or undue influence.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec71" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 71. Referral to Diversion Programs through the Katarungang Pambarangay. —<span class="section-top"> During the process of mediation or conciliation involving a child in conflict with the law under Section 23 of Republic Act No. 9344, the <i>Lupon Tagapamayapa</i> or the <i>Pangkat ng Tagapagkasundo</i> shall actively prioritize restorative outcomes over retributive measures.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Restorative Priority and Diversion Measures:</b> The <i>Lupon</i> or <i>Pangkat</i> shall encourage the inclusion of structured community-based diversion measures—such as community service, restitution of property, reparation of damage, formal written apology, or mandatory counseling—as part of the amicable settlement;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Integration as a Contract of Diversion:</b> Any diversion measure agreed upon during the conciliation process shall be explicitly incorporated into the written amicable settlement. Such settlement shall serve as the official Contract of Diversion for the child, provided it complies with the mandatory execution and content requirements under Section 69 and Section 70 of this Code; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Technical Assistance from the BCPC and LSWDO:</b> The <i>Lupon</i> or <i>Pangkat</i> shall seek the technical guidance and recommendations of the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC) and the Local Social Welfare and Development Officer (LSWDO) in formulating diversion plans. This ensures that the assigned measures are age-appropriate, non-hazardous, and tailored to the child’s rehabilitation and developmental capacity.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec72" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 72. Designation of Specialized Law Enforcement Officers. —<span class="section-top"> The Philippine National Police (PNP) Municipal Police Station of Libacao shall designate specialized units and trained personnel to manage cases involving Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL) and Children at Risk (CAR).</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Women and Children Protection Desk (WCPD):</b> A dedicated WCPD shall be maintained within the municipal police station. It shall be appropriately equipped to provide a private, child-friendly, and non-intimidating environment for initial contact, interview, and processing of children, completely separate from adult holding facilities;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Family, Juvenile, and Gender Specialist (FJGS):</b> The PNP station shall assign designated officers who have completed specialized training in child rights, juvenile justice management, human rights, and trauma-informed investigative techniques conducted or accredited by the Juvenile Justice and Welfare Council (JJWC) or the PNP;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Organizational Integration:</b> The FJGS may be integrated into the existing WCPD or established as a distinct operational unit, depending on the volume of cases and the administrative resources of the municipal police station; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Immediate Coordination with the MSWDO:</b> The designated law enforcement officers shall immediately notify and coordinate with the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO) upon taking custody of or making initial contact with a child. Pursuant to Section 21 of Republic Act No. 9344, as amended, the handling officer shall ensure the presence of a social worker during intake and ensure that the child's rights are strictly protected at all times.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec73" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 73. Procedure Before the Women and Children Protection Desk (WCPD). —<span class="section-top"> All cases involving a child in conflict with the law (CICL) shall be immediately referred to the Women and Children Protection Desk (WCPD) of the Libacao Municipal Police Station. The WCPD shall conduct an initial inquiry and handle the child in strict accordance with child-sensitive rules of judicial proceedings and the following mandatory custodial protocols:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Immediate Notification:</b> Upon the child being brought to the WCPD, the handling officer shall immediately and without delay notify the child’s parents or legal guardian, the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO), and the Public Attorney’s Office (PAO) or designated legal counsel;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Non-Adversarial Conduct of Inquiry:</b> The initial inquiry and intake process shall be conducted in a child-friendly, non-adversarial manner. The child shall not be subjected to any form of coercion, threat, or intimidation. Statements or interviews shall be taken only in the presence of the child’s legal counsel (or a PAO lawyer) and an assigned social worker from the MSWDO;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Strict Separation from Adults:</b> At no point during the intake, inquiry, or temporary custody shall the child be placed in a cell, holding room, or area where they can see, hear, or have any contact with adult detainees or offenders;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Mandatory Medical Examination:</b> The child shall be immediately referred to the Municipal Health Officer (MHO) or a government physician for a comprehensive physical, mental, and psychological examination to verify their well-being, assess age if necessary, and properly document any prior injuries; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e) Strict Observance of Custodial Time Limits:</b> The WCPD shall ensure that the child is not detained longer than the maximum period prescribed by law (Republic Act No. 9344, as amended) before being officially turned over to the MSWDO, a licensed youth care facility (<i>Bahay Pag-asa</i>), or released to their parents, as warranted by the case.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec74" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 74. Duty of the WCPD and Jurisdictional Referral. —<span class="section-top"> Following the initial inquiry and intake process, the Women and Children Protection Desk (WCPD) shall determine the appropriate legal venue and disposition for the case based on the nature and imposable penalty of the alleged offense:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Referral for Barangay Diversion:</b> Pursuant to Section 23 of Republic Act No. 9344, if the offense charged carries an imposable penalty of <b>not more than six (6) years imprisonment</b>, the WCPD shall immediately refer the case and transfer the child to the <i>Punong Barangay</i> or <i>Lupon Tagapamayapa</i> of the concerned barangay for appropriate mediation, conciliation, and community-based diversion;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Formal Investigation for Serious Offenses:</b> If the offense charged carries an imposable penalty exceeding six (6) years imprisonment, the WCPD shall not refer the case to the barangay. Instead, it shall proceed with a formal, child-sensitive investigation and prepare the necessary records for immediate transmittal to the Office of the Municipal Prosecutor;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Documentation and Monitoring of Referral:</b> In all cases of referral to the barangay, the WCPD shall issue a formal written referral document and ensure that the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC) and the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO) are duly notified to track and monitor the proceedings; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Release on Undertaking During Referral:</b> Pending the actual turnover or scheduling of barangay proceedings, the child shall not be subjected to prolonged police detention. The WCPD shall immediately release the child to the custody of their parents, legal guardian, or nearest relative, subject only to a simplified written undertaking to appear before the barangay authorities when summoned.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec75" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 75. Manner of Investigation of a Child in Conflict with the Law. —<span class="section-top"> The custodial investigation, interrogation, and the securing of statements from a child in conflict with the law (CICL) shall be conducted in a child-sensitive, non-adversarial manner and in strict compliance with the following constitutional and statutory safeguards:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Mandatory Presence of Adult Advocates and Counsel:</b> No custodial investigation shall proceed, and no statement, admission, or confession shall be secured from the child, unless conducted in the presence of their legal counsel and any of the following adult advocates, strictly observed in this order of preference:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	The parent(s) or legal guardian;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	The nearest relative;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	An accredited member of a child-focused or faith-based group;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">4.	A designated member of the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC) concerned; or</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">5.	The Local Social Welfare and Development Officer (LSWDO) or assigned social worker;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Comprehensible Notification of Rights:</b> Before any questioning or intake begins, the handling officer shall inform the child of their constitutional rights—including the right to remain silent and the right to counsel. These rights must be explained clearly and thoroughly in a language or dialect (such as Aklanon or Hiligaynon) that is fully understood by both the child and their parents, guardians, or assisting advocates;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Right to Competent and Independent Counsel:</b> The child shall be entitled to a competent and independent legal counsel of their own choice. If the child or their family cannot afford private legal services, the Women and Children Protection Desk (WCPD) shall immediately contact and coordinate with the Public Attorney’s Office (PAO) to assign a public defender to assist the child throughout the entire investigation process;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Strict Inadmissibility of Tainted Evidence:</b> Any confession, admission, or statement obtained from the child in violation of this section, or without the mandatory presence of legal counsel and an authorized adult advocate, shall be <i>null</i> and <i>void ab initio</i> and strictly inadmissible as evidence against the child in any administrative, civil, or criminal proceeding;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e) Absolute Prohibition of Coercion and Deceit:</b> In no case shall deceit, false promises, intimidation, threats, psychological pressure, tricks, or physical harassment be employed against the child to extract information or coerce a confession; and</p>		
					<p class="section-inner"><b>f) Paramount Consideration of the Child's Rights:</b> Absolute respect for the human rights and dignity of the child, as guaranteed by the Philippine Constitution, Republic Act No. 9344, as amended, and the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child (UNCRC), shall be the paramount consideration at all stages of the custodial investigation and legal processing.</p>						
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec76" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 76. Diversion in the Absence of a Private Offended Party. —<span class="section-top"> Where the offense committed involves no specific or private offended party (victimless or public-order offenses), and the child voluntarily gives consent to a diversion process, the Women and Children Protection Desk (WCPD)—in active consultation with the Local Social Welfare and Development Officer (LSWDO) and the child's parents or guardians—shall formulate an appropriate, restorative diversion program.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Core Consultative Group:</b> The formulation of the diversion program shall be led jointly by the WCPD and the LSWDO, with the mandatory participation and input of any of the following, as applicable:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	The child’s parents or legal guardians;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	The child’s nearest relative;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	A designated member of the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC) concerned; or</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">4.	An accredited representative from a child-focused or faith-based group active in the municipality;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Formalization and Referral:</b> Upon reaching a consensus, the terms of the diversion program shall be formalized into a written Contract of Diversion signed by all participating parties pursuant to Section 69 of this Code. The WCPD shall then formally refer the child to the appropriate municipal agencies, barangay units, or accredited local organizations for the implementation and monitoring of the program; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Focus of the Program:</b> Because there is no individual private victim to indemnify or compensate, the diversion program in these cases shall heavily prioritize community-based responses, such as values formation seminars, vocational or skills training, or localized community service projects that directly benefit the public welfare of the Municipality of Libacao.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec77" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 77. Right of the Child to Counsel. —<span class="section-top"> From the moment of initial police contact, throughout custodial investigation, and at every subsequent stage of administrative, diversion, or judicial proceedings, a Child in Conflict with the Law (CICL) shall have the absolute right to be represented by competent, independent, and effective legal counsel.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Duty of State Authorities:</b> Every arresting officer, investigating law enforcement officer, preliminary prosecutor, or presiding judge shall have the mandatory duty to ensure that the child is properly represented by counsel before commencing any interrogation, intake, preliminary investigation, or trial proceeding;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Mandatory Appointment of Counsel <i>de Oficio:</i></b> If the child or their parents/guardians are financially unable to retain private legal services, the authority handling the specific stage of the proceeding shall immediately halt all actions and secure the services of a public attorney from the Public Attorney’s Office (PAO) or an assigned competent counsel <i>de oficio</i> to assist the child;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Absolute Inadmissibility of Unrepresented Statements:</b> Any confession, admission, statement, or waiver made by the child at any stage of the proceedings without the active, effective, and continuous assistance of counsel shall be null and void ab initio and strictly inadmissible as evidence against the child for any purpose whatsoever; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Right to Confidential Consultation:</b> The child shall be afforded ample time and a private, secure space for confidential consultations with their legal counsel prior to and during any official interview, investigation, conference, or court proceeding.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec78" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 78. Custody and Supervision of the Child. —<span class="section-top"> No child shall be removed from parental care and supervision, whether partially or entirely, unless the specific circumstances of their case clearly warrant protective custody for the child's own safety, welfare, and best interest.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Primary Custody to Parents or Guardians:</b> The competent authorities conducting diversion proceedings shall immediately release and commit the child to the custody of their parents or legal guardians. The parents or guardians shall be legally responsible for ensuring the child’s presence throughout all stages of the diversion proceedings;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Alternative Community Custody:</b> In the event of the absence, unavailability, unfitness, or disqualification of the parents or legal guardians, the child shall be committed to the care and supervision of any of the following, observed in this strict order of preference:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	The nearest competent relative;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	An authorized member of the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC) concerned; or</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	The Local Social Welfare and Development Officer (LSWDO) or assigned social worker;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Institutional Referral as a Last Resort:</b> If community-based placement is impossible, or if the child’s safety and well-being are severely compromised, the LSWDO, in coordination with the Department of Social Welfare and Development (DSWD), may refer the child to an appropriate, accredited youth care institution or residential facility, such as a <i>Bahay Pag-asa</i>; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Strict Restriction on Detention Facilities:</b> Under no circumstances shall a child undergoing diversion proceedings, temporary custody, or intake be placed in a municipal jail, police lock-up cell, or any other detention facility intended for adult offenders.</p>
				</b></section><b>

				<section id="art6-sec79" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 79. Confidentiality of Records and Proceedings. —<span class="section-top"> All records, documents, communications, and proceedings involving a Child in Conflict with the Law (CICL) or a Child at Risk (CAR) shall be strictly confidential. To safeguard the child from public stigma, preserve their dignity, and protect their fundamental right to privacy, the following protocols shall be strictly observed:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Exclusion of the General Public:</b> The general public, media personnel, and unconcerned individuals shall be strictly excluded from all hearings, interviews, intakes, and proceedings. Only the parties directly involved, their legal counsel, assigned social workers, and authorized support individuals shall be permitted to attend;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Total Prohibition on Disclosure:</b> No participant in the proceedings—including law enforcement officers, social workers, barangay officials, medical personnel, or the parties themselves—shall disclose, directly or indirectly, any material information, name, or detail that could reveal the identity of the child to unauthorized persons or the media;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Administrative Safeguards for Law Enforcement:</b> The Libacao Municipal Police Station, through the Women and Children Protection Desk (WCPD), shall implement mandatory administrative safeguards:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Separate Police Blotter:</b> Maintain a separate, secure "pink blotter" or dedicated registry for cases involving children, which shall be physically and digitally kept away from the general adult police blotter; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Alphanumeric Coding System:</b> Adopt a strict alphanumeric coding system to replace the names and material identifying details of the child in all open administrative tracking logs or public-facing municipal records; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Sealing and Absolute Exclusion from Adult Proceedings:</b> Upon the successful completion of a diversion program, dismissal of the case, or final resolution, all records pertaining to the child shall be immediately closed and sealed. Such records shall never be published, cited, used, or admitted as evidence against the individual in any subsequent adult administrative, civil, or criminal proceedings.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec80" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 80. Care and Maintenance of a Child with a Suspended Sentence. —<span class="section-top"> The expenses incurred for the care, maintenance, rehabilitation, and training of a Child in Conflict with the Law (CICL) whose sentence has been suspended shall be borne primarily by their parents or persons legally liable for their support under the Family Code of the Philippines.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Equity and Subsidy for Indigent Families:</b> In cases where the parents or persons liable for support are financially incapable of paying all or part of the said expenses, as officially certified by the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO), the remaining cost shall be shared equally among the tiers of government in the following statutory proportions:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	One-Third (1/3) shall be paid by the Municipality of Libacao, chargeable against its annual allocation for child protection or MSWDO statutory funds;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	One-Third (1/3) shall be borne by the Province of Aklan; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">3.	One-Third (1/3) shall be borne by the National Government, through the Department of Social Welfare and Development (DSWD);</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Scope of Covered Expenses:</b> The expenses covered under this section shall include, but are not limited to, the child’s basic food, clothing, medical care, psychological intervention, professional counseling, and educational or vocational training fees while placed in an accredited youth care facility, residential center, or community-based rehabilitation program; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Administrative Allocation and Compliance:</b> The Municipal Budget Officer, Municipal Treasurer, and the MSWDO shall ensure that the necessary funds representing the Municipality’s one-third share are explicitly programmed, allocated, and made available under the local fiscal budget for juvenile welfare and child protection programs.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec81" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 81. Court-Ordered Reintegration Programs. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipal Government of Libacao, through the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO) and in close coordination with the Barangay Councils and the Local Council for the Protection of Children (LCPC), shall design, fund, and implement comprehensive community reintegration programs for Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL) who are officially released by the courts or completing diversion terms.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Purpose and Objective:</b> The primary objective of these programs is to facilitate a smooth, safe, and dignified transition of the child from judicial supervision or institutional care back into their family environment and local community, actively reducing the risk of recidivism and social stigmatization;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Comprehensive Reintegration Packages:</b> The reintegration program shall provide targeted, individualized assistance based on the assessed needs of the child, which shall include, when applicable:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Psycho-Social Counseling:</b> Continuous psychological support, therapy, and family counseling sessions designed to heal fractured household dynamics and prepare the home environment for the child’s return;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Educational Re-Enrollment:</b> Direct coordination with the Department of Education (DepEd) district supervisor and local schools to re-integrate the child into the formal school system or enroll them in the Alternative Learning System (ALS);</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>3.	Livelihood and Skills Training:</b> Providing older children and youth with access to vocational programs, such as TESDA-accredited courses, or local agricultural and abaca-weaving livelihood initiatives within the municipality; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>4.	Community Acceptance Campaigns:</b> Localized, sensitive awareness and mediation efforts led by the BCPC to reduce public stigma and ensure the child is safely welcomed back into community and barangay activities.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Post-Release Monitoring:</b> The MSWDO shall assign a dedicated caseworker or social worker to monitor the child’s progress, school attendance, and adjustment for a minimum period of six (6) months to one (1) year following their release from court supervision or completion of diversion.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec82" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">F. Child Safe Tourism</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 82. Integration of Child-Safe Tourism Protocols. —<span class="section-top"> To ensure that the growth and promotion of the local tourism industry in the Municipality of Libacao does not compromise the rights, safety, and moral development of minors, a comprehensive Child-Safe Tourism Protocol shall be established, maintained, and permanently incorporated into the Local Tourism Code of the Municipality.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Mandatory Tourism Guidelines:</b> The Municipal Tourism Office, in strict coordination with the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO) and the Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC), shall formulate and enforce rules requiring all tourism-related establishments (TREs)—including eco-tourism sites, inland resorts, homestays, hotels, transport operators, and tour guides—to adopt explicit child protection policies;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Zero-Tolerance for Exploitation:</b> The protocol shall strictly prohibit the employment of children in hazardous tourism labor and establish immediate, zero-tolerance reporting mechanisms and procedures for handling any suspected cases of child trafficking, tourism-related sexual exploitation, or abuse;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Capacity Building and Permitting Prerequisite:</b> Regular orientation and training on child rights, the detection of child trafficking vectors, and "Child-Safe Tourism" standards shall be a mandatory prerequisite for the issuance, compliance monitoring, or annual renewal of municipal business permits for all TREs operating within Libacao; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Public Awareness and Signage:</b> Child protection hotlines, warning signs against child exploitation, and informational materials shall be prominently and visibly displayed in all public tourism hubs, transport terminals, and registered hospitality establishments throughout the municipality.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art6-sec83" class="section-card">
					<h2><span class="section-top">G. Child in Emergency</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 83. Early Childhood Care and Development (ECCD) in Emergencies. —<span class="section-top"> To protect the rights, health, safety, and psychological well-being of young children during natural disasters, health crises, and other public emergencies, the Municipal Committee for the Protection of Children (MCPC), in coordination with the Municipal Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Office (MDRRMO) and the MSWDO, shall formulate and implement a comprehensive ECCD in Emergencies (ECCDiE) Plan.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Integration with Disaster Risk Reduction</b> The ECCDiE Plan shall be formally incorporated into the Municipal Disaster Risk Reduction and Management (MDRRM) Plan of Libacao to ensure seamless inter-agency coordination, immediate deployment of emergency resources, and institutional funding during a state of calamity;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Minimum Components of the ECCDiE Plan:</b> The plan shall mandate the establishment of child-centered emergency responses, which must include:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Child-Friendly Spaces (CFS):</b> Establishing safe, designated, and secure spaces within or near evacuation centers equipped with age-appropriate learning, play, and psychological first-aid materials specifically tailored for children aged zero (0) to eight (8) years;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Health and Nutrition Security:</b> Ensuring the immediate availability of clean water, emergency health services, and specialized nutritional support, including the strict promotion, protection, and support of breastfeeding and safe infant feeding in emergencies (IFE); and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>3.	Maternal and Caregiver Support:</b> Providing targeted psychosocial interventions, counseling, and safe hygiene spaces for pregnant women, lactating mothers, and primary caregivers.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Joint Budgetary Allocation:</b> The implementation, maintenance, and prepositioning of supplies for the ECCDiE Plan shall be co-funded by the Local Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Fund (Calamity Fund) and the annual statutory allocations of the MCPC and MSWDO, ensuring that child-specific relief goods, kits, and facilities are pre-positioned before disasters occur.</p>
				</section>

			</article>

			

			<!-- Article 7 Content -->
			<article id="art7" class="article-card">
				<h1 class="articleNumber"><b>ARTICLE 7</b></h1>
				<h1 class="articleTitle">IMPLEMENTING MECHANISMS</h1>
				<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>

				<section id="art7-sec84" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 84. Establishment and Composition of the Councils for the Protection of Children. —<span class="section-top"> The councils for the protection of children shall be operationalized at two distinct administrative tiers within the Municipality of Libacao to actively champion child survival, protection, development, and participation, and to execute the provisions of this Code:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p id="art7-sec84.A" class="section-p"><b>A. Local Councils for the Protection of Children</b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) The Municipal Council for the Protection of Children (MCPC):</b> The MCPC shall serve as the primary policy-making, coordinating, and monitoring body for child-focused initiatives at the municipal level. It shall be composed of the following:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Chairperson:</b> Municipal Mayor;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Vice-Chairperson:</b> Municipal Social Welfare and Development Officer (MSWDO); and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>3.	Members:</b></p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Chairperson, Sangguniang Bayan Committee on Women and Family;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	DILG Municipal Local Government Operations Officer (MLGOO);</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	District Supervisor, Department of Education (DepEd);</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Local Labor and Employment Officer;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Municipal Planning and Development Officer (MPDO);</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Municipal Budget Officer;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Municipal Health Officer (MHO);</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Municipal Nutrition Action Officer (MNAO);</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	PNP Chief of Police;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Municipal Treasurer;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	President, Liga ng mga Barangay (LnB) Libacao Chapter;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	President, Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) Municipal Federation;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Municipal Gender and Development (GAD) Focal Person;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	President, Municipal Parent-Teachers Association (PTA) Federation;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Child Representative; and</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	At least three (3) Representatives from accredited Civil Society Organizations (CSOs);</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) The Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC):</b> A functional BCPC shall be maintained within every barangay in the Municipality of Libacao as mandated under Section 15 of RA 9344. It shall serve as the frontline implementation unit and shall be composed of the following:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Chairperson:</b> Punong Barangay; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Members:</b></p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Barangay Kagawad (Chairperson, Committee on Women and Family);</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS);</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Barangay Day Care Worker(s);</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Barangay Health Worker (BHW);</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	DepEd School Principal or designated Teacher-in-Charge;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Chief Tanod;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) Chairperson;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Child Representative;</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Barangay PTA President or authorized representative; and</p>
							<p class="section-inner3">o	Accredited CSO / Faith-Based Group Representative.</p>

					<p id="art7-sec84.B" class="section-p"><b>B. Duties and Responsibilities of the MCPC</b></p>
					<p class="section-p">The MCPC shall perform the following core administrative and strategic functions:</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>1.	Comprehensive Juvenile Intervention Coordination:</b> Serve as the central coordinating authority to assist the Municipal Government in adopting, managing, and reviewing the Comprehensive Juvenile Intervention Program pursuant to Rule 18 of the Implementing Rules and Regulations (IRR) of RA 9344;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>2.	Multi-Sectoral Mobilization:</b> Mobilize child-focused institutions, CSOs, People's Organizations, educational networks, and state agencies to actively collaborate in planning and deploying delinquency prevention frameworks;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>3.	Annual Assessment:</b> Oversee the annual monitoring, evaluation, and data audit of the comprehensive local juvenile intervention and diversion initiatives;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>4.	Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) Technical Support:</b> Jointly coordinate with and provide technical guidance to the SK Municipal Federation and localized barangay youth councils in formulating responsive youth-led diversion initiatives;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>5.	Multi-Agency Linkages:</b> Bridge operational pathways across various judicial, administrative, and non-governmental institutions for tracking and evaluating local restorative justice mechanisms;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>6.	Grassroots Diversion Support:</b> Assist the Punong Barangay in managing and documenting localized diversion proceedings for cases falling under Section 23(a) of RA 9344;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>7.	MSWDO Collaboration:</b> Assist the MSWDO in designing nuanced, data-backed community-based diversion menus as provided under Section 23(b) of RA 9344;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>8.	Community-Based Program Instigation:</b> Collaborate with schools, local youth organizations, and barangay councils to establish localized, safe hubs for juvenile justice and welfare initiatives;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>9.	Capacity Building:</b> Facilitate regular training, capability building, and gender-responsive orientation programs for all local desk officers, caseworkers, and barangay officials handling children’s programs;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>10.	Data Management and Coding Systems:</b> Build and maintain a centralized, highly secure database tracking Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL) and Children at Risk (CAR), utilizing the alphanumeric coding protocols established under Section 79;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>11.	Institutional Documentation:</b> Document and archive local best practices regarding juvenile intervention, restorative justice, and delinquency prevention for continuous policy refinement;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>12.	Policy Advocacy and Legislative Recommendations:</b> Formulate and submit policy briefs and ordinance proposals to the Sangguniang Bayan aimed at protecting child survival and development, with specific emphasis on checking local media prints, public broadcasts, and digital exposure detrimental to minors;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>13.	CSO Resource Mapping:</b> Conduct a regular inventory of all active civil society organizations handling child welfare and mobilize them as supplementary resources for RA 9344 implementation;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>14.	Structural Barrier Elimination:</b> Review administrative service points across the municipality, identify legal or physical access barriers preventing vulnerable children from seeking help, and take direct corrective action to streamline municipal child-welfare access;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>15.	Execution Mandate:</b> Ensure the thorough enforcement, localized application, and monitoring of this Child Welfare Code across the Municipality of Libacao; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>16.	Development and Investment Planning:</b> Formulate, review, and update a three (3) year Local Development Plan for Children (LDPC) and a corresponding Local Investment Plan for Children (LIPC) to ensure structural compliance with the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child (UNCRC).</p>

					<p id="art7-sec84.C" class="section-p"><b>C. Duties and Responsibilities of the BCPC and its Members</b></p>
					<p class="section-p">In addition to its statutory obligations under Presidential Decree No. 603 (The Child and Youth Welfare Code) and Republic Act No. 8980 (The ECCD Act), the BCPC and its individual members shall perform the following frontline restorative and preventative tasks:</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Core Institutional Duties of the BCPC:</b></p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Parental Rearing Guidance:</b> Encourage the proper performance of parental obligations and conduct localized learning programs focused on positive parenting techniques and non-violent child-rearing;</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Expert Guidance Linkage:</b> Actively assist parents and guardians in securing expert counseling and psychological evaluation services from provincial, regional, or private social welfare institutions;</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>3.	Educational Materials and Seminars:</b> Conduct structural seminars on child guidance and maintain a readily accessible library of child welfare and positive discipline literature for distribution to parents;</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>4.	Youth Welfare Integration:</b> Coordinate all independent youth and child welfare organizations operating within the barangay, integrating their annual action plans with the Sangguniang Kabataan;</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>5.	Frontline Protection for Children at Risk:</b> Identify, map, and deploy immediate social safety nets for Children at Risk (CAR) within their respective jurisdictions; and</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>6.	Localized Delinquency Prevention:</b> Take immediate, proactive steps to mitigate localized delinquency risks, directly assisting families dealing with children exhibiting behavioral distress.</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Specialized Responsibilities of Individual BCPC Members:</b></p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Emergency Alternative Custody:</b> Take temporary, protective custody of a child in conflict with the law who is fifteen (15) years of age or below in cases where the parents, legal guardians, or nearest relatives cannot be located, or if they refuse to take custody, strictly adhering to Section 20 of RA 9344; and</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Witness and Advocate in Initial Investigation:</b> Act as the mandatory legal witness and advocate during the initial police contact or WCPD intake of a child in conflict with the law if the child's parents, guardians, or nearest relatives are absent, or if the MSWDO is unavailable, to guarantee that the child's constitutional right to silence and right to counsel are protected.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art7-sec85" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 85. Representation of Civil Society Organizations (CSO) in the Councils for Children. —<span class="section-top"> The local legislative councils shall invite CSOs with child-focused programs for membership in the council for the protection of children, provided that these non-government organizations are duly accredited by the local Sanggunian concerned based on its criteria of accreditation.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Mandatory Accreditation Prerequisite:</b> To qualify for a seat in the MCPC or BCPC, the inviting child-focused organization must be duly accredited by the Sangguniang Bayan of Libacao or the Sangguniang Barangay concerned, in strict accordance with the standard criteria and guidelines for LGU-CSO accreditation prescribed by law and the Department of the Interior and Local Government (DILG);</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Selection Process:</b> In the event that more than three (3) accredited child-focused CSOs express intent to join the MCPC, the accredited organizations shall conduct an independent selection process among themselves to choose their three (3) principal sector representatives, ensuring diverse expertise across child survival, development, and protection; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Term of Office:</b> The selected CSO representatives shall serve a term co-terminus with the local elective officials, without prejudice to their re-appointment or re-selection by their parent organizations upon renewal of their council seats.</p>
				</section>				

				<section id="art7-sec86" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 86. Internal Rules of Procedure of the Councils for the Protection of Children. —<span class="section-top"> Within sixty (60) days from the effectivity of this Code, or upon the reorganization of the councils following a local election, the Municipal Council for the Protection of Children (MCPC) and each Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC) shall formally adopt their own Internal Rules of Procedure (IRP). These rules shall serve as the official operational guidelines for members in the discharge of their functions and shall explicitly govern the following administrative matters:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Organizational Structure:</b> Defining the roles, duties, and functional sub-committees (such as committees on public advocacy, data tracking, or resource mobilization) within the council to streamline workflow and task allocation;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Meetings and Notice:</b> Establishing a regular schedule for meetings, which shall be held at least once every quarter, or as frequently as necessary for urgent or emergency cases, including protocols for calling special meetings and providing timely notices to all members;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Quorum and Parliamentary Procedures:</b> Defining the requirements for a valid quorum, which shall consist of a simple majority of all active members, and adopting standard parliamentary rules to govern debates, voting thresholds, and the resolution of motions;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Order of Business:</b> Setting a standardized agenda format for every meeting to ensure systematic tracking of committee reports, pending programs, budget reviews, and urgent child-protection updates;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>e) Discipline and Attendance:</b> Formulating internal rules regarding the behavior, ethical standards, and continuous attendance of members, including sanctions, reprimands, or mechanisms for replacing consistently inactive or non-compliant council members; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>f) Secretariat Support:</b> Designating a permanent secretariat team, led by the MSWDO at the municipal level and a designated barangay secretary or worker at the grassroots level, responsible for recording minutes, managing resolutions, and archiving confidential council records.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art7-sec87" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 87. Appointment of a Registered Social Worker for Juvenile Cases. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipal Government of Libacao shall designate or appoint at least one (1) full-time, licensed Registered Social Worker (RSW) within the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO) whose primary and specialized responsibility shall be to handle cases involving Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL) and Children at Risk (CAR).</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Minimum Professional Qualifications:</b> The designated officer must be a holder of a valid professional license issued by the Professional Regulation Commission (PRC) pursuant to Republic Act No. 4373 (The Social Work Law) and must have undergone specialized training in child rights, case management, counseling, and the juvenile justice system;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Core Functions and Duties:</b> The designated Registered Social Worker shall execute the following specialized tasks:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Intake and Assessment:</b> Conduct the immediate intake assessment of a child upon referral by law enforcement or the barangay, and determine the child's psychological, social, and developmental status;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Diversion Program Formulation:</b> Lead the formulation, implementation, and monitoring of customized community-based diversion programs;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>3.	Social Case Study Reports:</b> Prepare and submit comprehensive Social Case Study Reports (SCSR) to the courts, prosecutors, or the WCPD, containing data-backed recommendations on the best interest of the child; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>4.	Court Representation:</b> Act as the official advocate and representative of the local social welfare system during court hearings, trials, and administrative proceedings involving the child.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Case Load Management:</b> The MSWDO shall ensure that the designated social worker is provided with adequate administrative support to maintain a manageable case-to-worker ratio, ensuring that the quality of psycho-social intervention and monitoring for Libacao's youth is never compromised.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art7-sec88" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 88. Local Database on Children. —<span class="section-top"> A comprehensive, centralized, and secure database on children shall be established, maintained, and updated annually within the municipality to serve as the baseline for local development planning, policy formulation, and resource allocation.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Institutional Management and Responsibility:</b> The management of the database shall be split across the following administrative tiers:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Municipal Level:</b> The Municipal Planning and Development Office (MPDO), in close coordination with the MSWDO, shall maintain the consolidated Municipal Database on Children; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Barangay Level:</b> The respective Barangay Councils, through the BCPC Secretariat, shall maintain the localized Barangay Database on Children and submit quarterly updates to the MPDO;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Mandatory Data Parameters:</b> The database shall log, monitor, and categorize indicators for all minors residing within the jurisdiction, which shall include, but are not limited to, the following details:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Demographics:</b> Name, Age, Date of Birth, Sex, and Ethnicity, including affiliation with Indigenous Peoples or communities;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Socio-Economic and Educational Status:</b> Out-of-School Youth (OSY) status, current grade level, enrollment barriers, and household income bracket;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>3.	Special Protection Needs:</b> Children with Disabilities (CWD), orphaned or abandoned status, Children at Risk (CAR), and Children in Conflict with the Law (CICL) undergoing intervention or diversion; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>4.	Health Profiles:</b> Nutritional status, including indicators for stunting, wasting, and malnutrition, immunization records, and access to clean water.</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Strict Data Privacy Compliance:</b> In accordance with Republic Act No. 10173 (The Data Privacy Act of 2012) and Section 79 of this Code, the database shall be treated as highly sensitive:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">1.	Access to identifiable data shall be strictly limited to authorized social workers, municipal planners, and council executives; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2">2.	For general public presentation, budgetary allocations, or statutory reporting, all data must be fully anonymized, aggregated, and protected using the system of coding established under this Code.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art7-sec89" class="section-card">
					<h2 style="text-align: center;"><span class="section-top">DESK FOR CHILDREN’S CONCERNS</span></h2>
					<h2>SECTION 89. Creation of the Desk for Children’s Concerns. —<span class="section-top"> There is hereby created a permanent Desk for Children’s Concerns, which shall operate under the direct administrative supervision and control of the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO). The Municipal Government shall allocate the necessary personnel, equipment, and office space to ensure its full functionality.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></p>
						<p class="section-inner2"></p>
						<p class="section-inner2"></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art7-sec90" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 90. Functions of the Desk for Children’s Concerns. —<span class="section-top"> The Desk for Children’s Concerns shall serve as the primary administrative engine for municipal child advocacy and shall execute the following functions:</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Secretariat Support:</b> Act as the permanent, official Secretariat for the Municipal Council for the Protection of Children (MCPC), managing its archives, documentation, resolutions, and meeting alerts;</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Code Implementation and Monitoring:</b> Oversee, track, and coordinate the systematic implementation of all provisions contained within this Child Welfare Code across all municipal departments and barangays; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Day-to-Day Operations:</b> Manage daily administrative operations, stakeholder correspondence, and urgent emergency intake referrals for the MCPC.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art7-sec91" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 91. Mandatory Consultations on Child-Related Legislation. —<span class="section-top"> The Desk for Children’s Concerns shall be officially consulted by the Sangguniang Bayan, local government departments, and other statutory bodies whenever they are considering proposals to change, amend, or repeal existing ordinances, or when introducing new local legislation likely to affect the lives, safety, or well-being of children within the municipality.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></p>
						<p class="section-inner2"></p>
						<p class="section-inner2"></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art7-sec92" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 92. Reporting Obligations and Inter-Barangay Coordination. —<span class="section-top"> The Desk for Children’s Concerns shall serve as the central reviewing authority for all institutional compliance reporting regarding the rights of the child.</span></h2>
					<h2><span class="section-top"></span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner">a) The Desk shall guide, review, and evaluate the municipality's reporting obligations submitted to provincial, national, and international oversight bodies; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner">b) All twenty-four (24) component barangays of the Municipality of Libacao are strictly required to consult and submit their respective child status reports to the Desk during their local drafting phases, and they shall give due, documented consideration to the Desk’s technical comments and corrections.</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"></p>
						<p class="section-inner2"></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art7-sec93" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 93. Requirement for Child Impact Statements. —<span class="section-top"> All local government departments, committees, and proponents of local policy are hereby required to prepare and attach a formal "Child Impact Statement" to any proposed ordinance, resolution, or major infrastructure policy before its passage. This statement must explicitly analyze the potential direct and indirect repercussions of the proposed policy on children and must be submitted to the Desk for Children’s Concerns for vetting and clearance.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art7-sec94" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 94. Institutional Child Representation. —<span class="section-top"> To ensure the institutionalization of genuine child participation in local governance, a dedicated, voting seat shall be provided for an official Child Representative within the MCPC and in every BCPC. This seat shall exist independently from the youth seats reserved for the Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) and the Pag-asa Youth Association of the Philippines (PYAP), Inc. The municipal and barangay councils shall provide an enabling, child-sensitive environment to ensure that the Child Representative is able to genuinely participate in decision-making processes, policy debates, and budget allocations.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>
				</section>

				<section id="art7-sec95" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 95. Selection Process and Criteria for Child Representatives. —<span class="section-top"> The selection of the municipal and barangay child representatives shall strictly adhere to the standards outlined in the Guidebook on Child Participation developed by the National Committee on Child and Youth Participation (NCCYP), observed through the following operational phases and criteria:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) The Municipal Selection Assembly:</b> All duly selected BCPC Child Representatives from the various barangays shall convene annually in a Municipal Child Assembly. This assembly shall function independently to democratically select their principal representative, who shall then sit as the official, recognized voice of the children's sector in the MCPC; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Minimum Qualifications and Criteria:</b> Any child seeking selection as a representative to the BCPC or MCPC must strictly meet the following standards:</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Age Bracket:</b> Must be between ten (10) to fifteen (15) years of age during their term;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Community Engagement:</b> Must be an active, verified participant in community-led activities;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>3.	Sectoral Affiliation:</b> Must be an active member of an accredited or recognized youth organization, school group, student council, or faith-based youth association operating within the barangay;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>4.	Articulacy and Advocacy:</b> Must demonstrate the ability to clearly articulate children’s issues, rights, and peer concerns;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>5.	Residency:</b> Must have been a continuous resident of the respective barangay for at least one (1) year;</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>6.	Leadership Potential:</b> Must possess verifiable leadership skills, teamwork capacity, and peer respect; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner2"><b>7.	Inclusivity and Balance:</b> Must demonstrate balanced, non-discriminatory views regarding gender equality, religious freedom, cultural ethnicity, and the rights of Children with Disabilities (CWD).</p>
				</section>

			</article>

			<!-- Article 8 Content -->
			<article id="art8" class="article-card">
				<h1 class="articleNumber"><b>ARTICLE 8</b></h1>
				<h1 class="articleTitle">REMEDIAL MEASURES AND PENAL PROVISIONS</h1>

				<section id="art8-sec96" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 96. Persons Who May File a Complaint. —<span class="section-top"> Complaints regarding any unlawful acts, neglect, or abuse committed against children as defined in this Code or under applicable national laws may be formally filed by any of the following parties:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner">a) The offended child, with or without the assistance of an adult;</p>
					<p class="section-inner">b) The parents, legal guardians, or individuals exercising substitute parental authority;</p>
					<p class="section-inner">c) Ascendants or collateral relatives of the child within the third (3rd) degree of consanguinity or affinity;</p>
					<p class="section-inner">d) Social Welfare Officers of the Department of Social Welfare and Development (DSWD) or any Registered Social Worker of the Local Government Unit (LGU);</p>
					<p class="section-inner">e) The Punong Barangay of the locality where the violation occurred; or</p>
					<p class="section-inner">f) At least three (3) concerned, responsible citizens residing in the community or barangay where the violation took place.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art8-sec97" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 97. Immediate Protective Custody. —<span class="section-top"> Upon the filing of a complaint or detection of imminent danger, the offended child shall be immediately placed under the protective custody of the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Office (MSWDO) pursuant to Executive Order No. 56, series of 1986.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Immunity from Liability:</b> In the regular and good-faith performance of this protective function, the Municipal Social Welfare and Development Officer (MSWDO) and assisting social workers shall be completely free and immune from administrative, civil, or criminal liability; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Standard of Proceedings:</b> All custody, rescue, and placement proceedings shall be strictly carried out in accordance with the child-sensitive provisions of Presidential Decree No. 603 (The Child and Youth Welfare Code) and Republic Act No. 7610.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art8-sec98" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 98. Confidentiality and Anti-Sensationalism Guidelines. —<span class="section-top"> To safeguard the moral development, mental health, and social standing of the child, their identity must be shielded from public exposure:</span></h2>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>a) Sealing of Identity:</b> The name, address, school, and any identifying features of the offended party shall be strictly withheld from the public and media channels at least until a court of competent jurisdiction formally acquires jurisdiction over the case, subject to the permanent confidentiality filters mandated under Section 79; and</p>
					<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Prohibition on Sensationalism:</b> It shall be unlawful for any editor, publisher, reporter, photojournalist, or columnist (in the case of print and digital media); any announcer, host, or producer (in the case of television, radio, and digital broadcasting); or any producer and director (in the case of the film and entertainment industry) to cause undue, malicious, or sensationalized publicity of any case involving a violation of this Code that results in the moral degradation, psychological trauma, or public humiliation of the offended child.</p>
				</section>

				<section id="art8-sec99" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 99. Voluntary Reporting of Child Abuse. —<span class="section-top"> Any individual who learns of facts, signs, or circumstances that give rise to a reasonable belief that a child has suffered or is suffering from abuse, exploitation, or severe neglect may report such incidents, either orally or in writing, to the MSWDO, the Women and Children Protection Desk (WCPD) of the local PNP, any law enforcement agency, or the concerned Barangay Council for the Protection of Children (BCPC).</span></h2>
				</section>

				<section id="art8-sec100" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 100. Mandatory Reporting by Medical Institutions and Practitioners. —<span class="section-top"> The heads of hospitals, medical clinics, rural health units (RHUs), and similar healthcare institutions, as well as attending physicians, nurses, midwives, and medical technicians, shall have the mandatory duty to report, either orally or in writing, the examination, admission, or treatment of any child who appears to have suffered from physical, emotional, or sexual abuse. This report must be submitted to the MSWDO or the WCPD within forty-eight (48) hours from the time of medical knowledge or intake.</span></h2>
				</section>

				<section id="art8-sec101" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 101. Mandatory Reporting Duty of Government Workers. —<span class="section-top"> It shall be the mandatory duty of all teachers, instructors, and administrators in public and private schools; probation officers; government lawyers; law enforcement officers; barangay officials, including Kagawads, Tanods, and daycare workers; correction officers; and all other municipal officials and employees whose daily work involves direct dealing or interaction with children, to immediately report any indicators or clear suspicions of child abuse to the appropriate authorities designated under this Chapter.</span></h2>
				</section>

				<section id="art8-sec102" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 102. Sanctions for Failure to Report. —<span class="section-top"> Any professional or institutional worker listed under Section 100 and Section 101 who possesses clear knowledge or direct evidence of child abuse and willfully or negligently fails to report the case to the proper authorities within the prescribed period shall be held criminally and administratively liable, and shall be prosecuted and penalized under the penal provisions of Republic Act No. 7610.</span></h2>
				</section>

				<section id="art8-sec103" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 103. Statutory Immunity for Good-Faith Reporting. —<span class="section-top"> Any person who, acting in good faith, reports a case of child abuse or testifies in a proceeding relative thereto, shall be completely free and immune from any civil, criminal, or administrative liability arising from such report or testimony. There shall exist a standing legal presumption that any such reporter acted in good faith, in line with the principles of Republic Act No. 6981 (Witness Protection, Security and Benefit Act).</span></h2>
				</section>

				<section id="art8-sec104" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 104. Penalties and Restorative Alternative Measures. —<span class="section-top"> Any violation of the provisions of this Ordinance that is not explicitly penalized or covered by a higher penalty under national laws shall be punished by a fine and/or imprisonment within the maximum legal allowances permitted for municipalities:</span></h2>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>a) General Municipal Penalty:</b> Confinement in the municipal jail for a period not exceeding six (6) months, or a fine not exceeding Two Thousand Five Hundred Pesos (₱2,500.00), or both, at the absolute discretion of the court;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>b) Restorative Caveat for First-Time Intrafamilial Offenders:</b> For first-time offenders—in cases where the offender is a parent, legal guardian, school teacher, or relative of the child within the fourth (4th) degree of consanguinity or affinity—if the offender fully admits to the infraction, demonstrates deep, verifiable sincerity, and is willing to undergo rehabilitation, the court may choose to suspend or waive the punitive penalty of jail time and fine. This suspension shall be subject to the formal consent of the child and the mandatory completion of the following alternative measures:</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>1.	Mandatory continuous attendance</b> in positive parenting seminars, anger management courses, or localized behavioral counseling;</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>2.	Compulsory community service</b> within the Municipality of Libacao for a period of ten (10) to fifteen (15) days;</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>3.	Structured social worker</b> counseling sessions;</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>4.	Family therapy sessions</b> supervised by the MSWDO; and/or</p>
							<p class="section-inner2"><b>5.	Active participation</b> in appropriate livelihood or specialized capacity-training courses;</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>c) Supremacy of National Laws:</b> For offenses that are concurrently punishable under this Code and specific national statutes—such as R.A. 7610 (Special Protection Against Child Abuse), R.A. 9262 (Anti-Violence Against Women and Their Children Act), R.A. 7658 (Child Labor Prohibitions), or the Revised Penal Code—the offended party or authorized representative shall file the case under the national law, and the heavier penalties prescribed by the national state shall take strict precedence; and</p>
						<p class="section-inner"><b>d) Administrative Dereliction of Duty:</b> Failure or willful refusal to enforce and comply with the provisions of this Code by any concerned local chief executive, members of local legislative bodies, heads of offices, or barangay captains shall constitute Dereliction of Duty and Gross Neglect of Duty, rendering them subject to administrative sanctions, suspension, or dismissal under the Local Government Code of 1991 (R.A. 7160) and civil service regulations.</p>
				</section>

			</article>

			<!-- Article 9 Content -->
			<article id="art9" class="article-card">
				<h1 class="articleNumber"><b>ARTICLE 9</b></h1>
				<h1 class="articleTitle">BUDGETARY APPROPRIATIONS</h1>
				<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>

				<section id="art9-sec105" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 105. Budgetary Appropriations for Children’s Programs. —<span class="section-top"> The Municipality of Libacao shall appropriate funds for children aside from the mandatory allocation of one percent (1%) for the protection of children to ensure the sustainability of the programs, services, and facilities for children. Fund sources may be drawn from the Gender and Development (GAD) budget, the General Fund, and other available local revenue sources.</span></h2>
					<p class="section-p">The Sangguniang Kabataan (SK) at the barangay level are likewise encouraged to share a portion of their allowable budget under existing laws to support municipal and barangay children’s programs and projects.</p>
				</section>

			</article>

			<!-- Article 10 Content -->
			<article id="art10" class="article-card">
				<h1 class="articleNumber"><b>ARTICLE 10</b></h1>
				<h1 class="articleTitle">SEPARABILITY/ APPLICABILITY/ REPEALING EFFECTIVITY CLAUSES</h1>
				<p class="section-inner"><b></b></p>

				<section id="art10-sec106" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 106. Separability Clauses. —<span class="section-top">	If for any reason, any part or provision of this Code shall be declared/ deemed to be unconstitutional or invalid, other parts or provisions hereof which are not affected thereby shall remain effective;</span></h2>
				</section>

				<section id="art10-sec107" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 107. Applicability Clause. —<span class="section-top"> All other matters relating to the impositions or regulations provided in this Code shall be governed by the pertinent provisions of existing laws and other ordinances. This Code shall apply to all the people within the territorial jurisdiction of the Municipality of Libacao and all persons, who may be subject to the provision of this code. </span></h2>
				</section>

				<section id="art10-sec108" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 108. Repealing Clause. —<span class="section-top"> All ordinances, resolutions executive orders and other issuances which are inconsistent with any of the provisions of this Code are hereby repealed or modified accordingly.</span></h2>
				</section>

				<section id="art10-sec109" class="section-card">
					<h2>SECTION 109. Effectivity Clause. —<span class="section-top"> This Code shall take effect after completion of its publication in a newspaper of general circulation within its jurisdiction and posting in at least three conspicuous places in the Municipality of Libacao, Aklan.</span></h2>
				</section>
			</article>

